2,0,0,0,20.73752622065062401812700771390162,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\nbase_collection_funding_stress,True,2031-07-19,receipts_after_requirements,20.73752622065062401812700771390162,21.59876506592300269045291220684997,0,0,0,21.59876506592300269045291220684997,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\nbase_collection_funding_stress,True,2031-08-19,requirements_before_receipts,21.59876506592300269045291220684997,21.20209839925633602378624554018331,0,0,0,21.20209839925633602378624554018331,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\nbase_collection_funding_stress,True,2031-08-19,receipts_after_requirements,21.20209839925633602378624554018331,22.06337548545119089608856987552215,0,0,0,22.06337548545119089608856987552215,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\nbase_collection_funding_stress,True,2031-09-19,requirements_before_receipts,22.06337548545119089608856987552215,21.66670881878452422942190320885549,0,0,0,21.66670881878452422942190320885549,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\nbase_collection_funding_stress,True,2031-09-19,receipts_after_requirements,21.66670881878452422942190320885549,22.52802398656467831753407897057487,0,0,0,22.52802398656467831753407897057487,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\nbase_collection_funding_stress,True,2031-10-19,requirements_before_receipts,22.52802398656467831753407897057487,22.13135731989801165086741230390821,0,0,0,22.13135731989801165086741230390821,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\nbase_collection_funding_stress,True,2031-10-19,receipts_after_requirements,22.13135731989801165086741230390821,22.99271041059019287472356511106487,0,0,0,22.99271041059019287472356511106487,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\nbase_collection_funding_stress,True,2031-10-19,terminal_capital_catchup,22.99271041059019287472356511106487,22.99271041059019287472356511106487,0,0,0,22.99271041059019287472356511106487,15516763,0.000,False,conditional_no_rescue_funded\\r\\n\"","\"# R.K. Fashion Accessories Limited\\n\\nNSE Emerge: RKFAL · ISIN INE2O2Y01016 · Master Prompt v3.0\\n\\nInformation cutoff: 20 October 2026, India time. Valuation reference: official NSE close on 19 October 2026, ₹68.60. Research assembled later using only documents available by the cutoff. This is an evidence-limited deep-research dossier, not a completed clean-audit assurance, a buy instruction or an executed trade.\\n\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\nR.K. Fashion is a regional imitation-jewellery design, outsourcing and wholesale-distribution business, with cosmetics resale and hotel rental income. It is not yet an established owner-operated jewellery manufacturer: the first proposed in-house plating plant remains an execution project. Revenue and operating profit expanded sharply, but the strongest counterweight is cash: cumulative FY24–FY26 operating cash flow is negative despite positive reported profits in every year. Inventory, customer credit, property spending and promoter funding have grown together. At ₹68.60, the headline P/E understates the normalized P/E, while obscuring post-IPO capital risks. Classification: Hold — unresolved financial reliability, funding and tradability. BQS, GQS and IAS remain NS, with mathematical bounds rather than invented point scores. This is a speculative expansion/special-situation thesis, not an evidence-backed compounder. Reconciled original audits, verified IPO deployment and bank cash, collections and credible showroom/plant economics would be needed to change that conclusion. [S1, S2; A01–A09]\\n\\nThe conditional earned-value base produces a five-year CAGR of -4.06%; the bull requires hypothetical rescue and produces 1.75%. Those are analytical conditionals, not supported forecasts. Higher market-multiple outcomes shown later must not be mistaken for cash-earned intrinsic value.\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\nThe legal issuer was incorporated as Satabadi Distributor Private Limited in March 2010, renamed in February 2011 and converted to a public company in March 2026. The company has no holding company, subsidiaries or associates in the financial note reviewed. The report therefore uses standalone Indian GAAP Accounting Standards and Schedule III Division I, not an assumed Ind AS perimeter. FY24 includes merged businesses; FY25 includes two packaging proprietorship takeovers. The last audited/restated financial observation is 30 June 2026, not the valuation date. There is no comparable prior-June period from which to construct a defensible TTM. [S1 pp237–242, 290–314]\\n\\nNSE's 9 October listing circular and post-issue shareholding verify 15,516,763 issued fully paid shares, ₹82 issue price, listing from 12 October, ST trade-for-trade series and a 1,600-share trading lot. IPO application minimums are not confused with the secondary-market trading lot. The shares offered were 4,267,200, all fresh issue; no OFS. The five-year model runs from 19 October 2026 to 19 October 2031. The central equity hurdle is an analytical 15% nominal annual return before investor taxes; 12% and 18% are shown alongside it. ₹5 lakh is only the framework's illustrative liquidity reference, not a suitable allocation recommendation. [S2–S4]\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | Conditional | No substantiated diversion or minority abuse found; RPT, governance history and financial inconsistencies require reconciliation. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Original statutory/CARO/IFC unavailable, noncash merger and takeover lines in cash flow, conflicting merger/counterparty numbers; no reliable current investment conclusion. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | Actual entry cash/net proceeds/deployment/promoter-loan terms unverified. Conditional stress cash shortfall is not evidence of actual default. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | Six possible listed sessions and only three trade rows; 20/60-day implementation test unavailable, ST and 1,600-lot restrictions. |\\n\\n\\nConfidence is High for the current security identity, official close and post-issue share count; Medium for transcribed restated P&L, balance-sheet and cash-flow values; Low for organic growth, matched incremental returns, current cash and investment attractiveness. Overall confidence is Low because the unresolved areas are material. The financial examiner's statement about no qualifications requiring adjustments is not a substitute for the original statutory audit, CARO or internal-financial-control reports.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\nNS means not scoreable, not zero or an average score. Known factors retain their original weights. Bounds below are mathematical missing-data bounds, not confidence intervals. No score is rank-eligible while critical evidence and gates remain unresolved. Decimal arithmetic is retained before one-decimal half-up display. The exact original weights and their contributions are reproduced below.\\n\\n| Measure | Known weighted sum | Coverage | Raw/final | Raw mathematical bound | Effective bound |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | 2.70 | 68.00% | NS | 3.02–5.90 | 3.02–5.90 |\\n| GQS | 1.80 | 45.00% | NS | 2.35–7.30 | 2.35–5.00 |\\n| IAS | 0.90 | 30.00% | NS | 1.60–7.90 | 1.60–7.90 |\\n\\n\\nCASH = 2 reflects persistent negative multi-year realization, not simply one quarter of showroom stocking. It triggers the framework's GQS ceiling of 5. Because GQS is already NS, the result remains NS, not a point score of 5. There is no governance override: adverse evidence is not sufficient to allege serious abuse or assign GOV ≤3. There is no actual funding-failure override: hypothetical cash breaches do not prove the issuer defaulted. IAS is NS independently because BQS, R and L are not scoreable.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence and contrary fact |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | No OFS; IPO all primary capital and no pledge/convertibles in 9 Oct shareholding [S2]. Family-related funding and transactions are disclosed; no auditor change in preceding 3 years [S1 p 88]. Contrary: noncash financing and related balances conflict; newly constituted independent oversight has no long listed record [A02,A03,A05]. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | Regional wholesale relationships and branded design/quality coordination exist [S1 pp 197-227]. Top 10 FY26 customers 15.15%; no exclusive Karigar/noncompete and no committed order book [S1 pp 40-49]. Contrary: 12-15% pricing action and strong jewellery revenue growth show some commercial franchise; durable excess returns and switching costs are unverified. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | Reported FY24-FY26 revenue rose 1328.49→3035.71 lakh, only 2 annual intervals [S1 p 291]. FY24 merger, FY25 packaging takeovers and Aug 25 price rise obscure organic same-perimeter demand [A03;S1 p 339]. No reliable 3/5 year organic CAGR, volumes, same-store sales or acquired contribution; do not award 9 from nominal 2 year CAGR. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | Additional B2B showroom and B2C floors provide concrete distribution avenues; plating design 8064 kg/year [S1 pp 145-157]. West Bengal 65.77% and eastern states 86.32% FY26; no orders or independent plant appraisal [S1 pp 39-49,131]. Contrary: affordable imitation can substitute expensive gold, but accessible share/units and showroom mature cohorts are ND. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | Incremental finance-side capital proxy and normalized operating earnings can be calculated [S1 pp 290-303]. Property investment, acquired businesses and proposed 8.8037 crore plating facility do not provide matched project returns [A02,A03,S1 p 145]. Accounting-return proxy is not a verified incremental cohort ROIC; cost per kg, volumes, mix, unit contribution and sustainable maintenance capex missing. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Medium | Three audited/restated annual CFO sums negative 90.79 lakh against PAT 923.19 lakh [S1 p 292]. Receivable ageing and inventory cycle worsen into June; supplier credit no longer offsets growth [A04]. Contrary: showroom stocking has a stated commercial reason and IPO funds it, but negative conversion persists over 3 years; not merely one transient quarter. |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | June cash 0.2495 crore vs promoter loan 3.064 crore; no bank line [S1 pp 300,353]. IPO gross 34.99104 crore verified from shares and issue price; objects absorb 27.4681 crore before issue costs/general purposes [S2,S1 pp 129-130]. Current post-IPO bank balance, exact net proceeds, deployment and promoter loan due dates are not verified; projections are not funding commitments. |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | B2C ground floor opened April 2026 and existing B2B extended;37 new customers reported FY26 [S1 pp 133,334]. IPO completed 12 Oct and expansion has dated civil/retail schedule [S2,S1 pp 149-156]. Contrary: plant approvals/machine orders and showroom definitive acquisition pending at RHP date; no long promises-vs-delivery record. |\\n| EARN | 4 | Medium | Core operating EBIT improved from loss FY24 to profit FY25/FY26 [S1 p 291]. FY26 other income 1.1846 crore includes 1.1795 crore investment gain; FY24 similarly gain-dependent [S1 p 301]. Contrary: FY26 normalized operating profit remains positive, but price/mix/margins changed and post-IPO shares dilute historical EPS; June cannot be annualized. |\\n| R | NS | Low | Complete monthly conditional model includes capital requirements and funded/unfunded branches. Actual entry cash and final net proceeds are not known; conditional returns are not supported forecasts. No favorable return score may be assigned by replacing unknown capital position with optional finance. |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | Linked bear combines weaker sales/margins, aged collections, opening inventory and delayed overbudget plant. Cash-floor breach can precede annual profit recognition; bounded optional equity can run out. Contrary: fresh IPO capital and tangible property give potential protection, but restricted uses and illiquid assets do not guarantee recovery. |\\n| P | 3 | Medium | Current price 68.60 implies marketcap 106.44499 crore; normalized earnings multiple exceeds reported multiple. Reverse/hurdle solutions demand stronger realization or terminal economics than conservative cash-linked base. Contrary: a high-execution branch can create value, but not enough evidence for an expectation score above mixed. |\\n| L | NS | Low | Only 6 possible exchange sessions since 12 Oct; actual issuer rows only 12,13,19 Oct [S3]. 19 Oct turnover 109760 rupees and 1600 shares; stock ST trade-for-trade and 1600 lot [S2-S4]. 20/60 session means, median including verified zero days, spread and executable depth unavailable; pre-listing dates are NA, absent rows ND. |\\n\\n\\n| Measure | Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | GOV | 0.20 | 4 | 0.80 |\\n| BQS | MOAT | 0.20 | 4 | 0.80 |\\n| BQS | REV | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | DUR | 0.10 | 5 | 0.50 |\\n| BQS | CAP | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | CASH | 0.10 | 2 | 0.20 |\\n| BQS | BAL | 0.08 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | EXEC | 0.08 | 5 | 0.40 |\\n| GQS | REV | 0.40 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | DUR | 0.20 | 5 | 1.00 |\\n| GQS | EARN | 0.15 | 4 | 0.60 |\\n| GQS | CAP | 0.15 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | CASH | 0.10 | 2 | 0.20 |\\n| IAS | BQS | 0.25 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | R | 0.35 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | D | 0.20 | 3 | 0.60 |\\n| IAS | P | 0.10 | 3 | 0.30 |\\n| IAS | L | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n\\n\\nOne-point uncertainty tests preserve missing-data weights. A hypothetical GOV fall from 4 to 3 activates the framework’s BQS/IAS cap at 3; it is not a finding of substantiated misconduct in this case.\\n\\n| Factor change | BQS raw bound | BQS effective bound | IAS effective bound | Governance override |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV -1 | 2.82–5.70 | 2.82–3.00 | 1.60–3.00 | True |\\n| GOV 1 | 3.22–6.10 | 3.22–6.10 | 1.60–7.90 | False |\\n| MOAT -1 | 2.82–5.70 | 2.82–5.70 | 1.60–7.90 | False |\\n| MOAT 1 | 3.22–6.10 | 3.22–6.10 | 1.60–7.90 | False |\\n\\n\\nMoving GOV and MOAT together by one point changes the BQS known weighted contribution by 0.40 in either direction; it does not fill the missing 32% evidence weight. Uncertainty therefore dominates tiny apparent ranking differences. REV is not assigned from the two-year reported CAGR, and the missing organic evidence is not replaced with a neutral score.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nCity Girl and Manikya cover wedding, regular-wear and imitation CZ/AD jewellery. The company coordinates design, contract manufacturers, plating, quality checks, packaging and distribution; Karigars source and work on metal mountings. The RHP calls the jewellery line manufactured goods, but the economics are outsourced manufacturing, not simple gold-jewellery resale and not an owned integrated factory. Cosmetics are purchased branded goods for trading. The third segment comprises hotel-related services, commission and rents; FY24 included food/lodging while subsequent periods emphasize leasing. [S1 pp197–227, 300–312]\\n\\nFY26 segment revenues are ₹24.8572 crore jewellery, ₹4.9026 crore cosmetics and ₹0.5973 crore hotel-related income. In April–June 2026, jewellery is ₹7.3586 crore, cosmetics zero and rentals ₹0.1342 crore. Do not interpret zero quarterly cosmetics revenue as confirmed permanent discontinuation. The June operating and channel tables do not all use the same denominator. The audited/restated AS17 segment table is the anchor; inconsistent marketing tables are not forced into a fictitious exact driver bridge. [S1 pp301, 312; A07]\\n\\nFY26 wholesale contributed 82.53% versus 55.99% in FY24; June wholesale is 90.66%. The strategy says B2C expansion, but realized mix has moved toward wholesale so far. FY26 West Bengal supplied 65.77% of revenue and four eastern states 86.32%; June eastern concentration rose to 91.71%. FY26 top-five customers are 11.11% and top-ten 15.15%, meaning geographic and channel concentration are more acute than a single named customer's dominance. Counterparties are anonymized; connectedness cannot be independently ruled out. The FY25 top-ten amount divided by total revenue is 23.20%, inconsistent with the printed 20.24%. [S1 pp39–48]\\n\\nSales to export merchants are not equivalent to direct foreign-currency export revenue. The customer-type table shows FY26 ₹1.3148 crore to export merchants against its own ₹28.291 crore denominator, while the geography table describes domestic states. Import CIF and foreign-exchange expenditure are nil in the notes; no foreign-currency borrowing was identified. Direct export destinations, invoice currencies and hedging are ND. Currency risk is principally an indirect commodity/input-price exposure on the evidence available, not a verified dollar-revenue hedge. [S1 pp215, 307–308]\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nThe plausible advantage is local distribution, familiar designs, replenishment breadth and quality coordination. Relationships, a six-month colour guarantee and frequent collections may help repeat orders. They do not prove consumer brand pricing power, switching costs, exclusive production or superior lifetime customer economics. Karigar agreements are short, nonexclusive and have no noncompete. The company produces against demand expectations, not binding long-term order commitments. FY26 top-ten contract manufacturers supplied 35.46% of the stated purchases and June 61.37%, while the broader supplier table uses another denominator; these are separate populations, not interchangeable concentration ratios. [S1 pp41–49, 211–227]\\n\\nThe peer cross-check is economic, not a copied sector P/E target:\\n\\n- Banaras Beads is the RHP's listed comparison and has beads/fashion-accessory manufacturing and wholesale/retail-customer exposure. Its export orientation and product economics differ from a Kolkata imitation-jewellery distributor. The RHP itself says strict comparability is limited. [S1 pp165–166; P1]\\n- Vaibhav Global is a useful distribution/control benchmark because it has international TV/digital retail and vertically integrated fashion-jewellery operations. Its owned channels, customer data, geography and scale are materially different. It is not a valid plug-in multiple. [P2]\\n- Goldiam illustrates a different jewellery model: export-led natural/lab-grown diamond products and customer-held consignment inventory. Its metal value, foreign exposure and working-capital risks differ; its operating margins do not validate RK's margins. [P3]\\n\\nNo current peer valuation ranking is claimed. Promotional claims of national leadership or a large total market do not establish RK's accessible share. There is no evidence here for MOAT ≥7.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\nReported operations revenue rose from ₹13.2849 crore in FY24 to ₹17.7719 crore in FY25 and ₹30.3571 crore in FY26. Annual additions were ₹4.4870 crore and ₹12.5852 crore. Only three annual observations exist, hence only two growth intervals. A three-year CAGR requires four observations; five and ten years require six and eleven. Those endpoints were not located. The mathematically valid two-year reported CAGR is shown in the calculation table, not mislabeled organic or five-year growth. [S1 p291]\\n\\nThe bridge is not purely volume. Jewellery grew ₹12.0388 crore in FY26, cosmetics ₹0.4670 crore and hotel-related income ₹0.0794 crore, within source rounding. Management attributes growth to customer additions, mix and a 12–15% selling-price increase from August 2025. It reports 37 new customers and ₹5.5116 crore from those customers in FY26, but that amount is not automatically the incremental revenue versus the prior year because existing and new cohorts have different comparison bases. Unit volumes, weight per item and like-for-like store sales are unavailable. [S1 pp334–339]\\n\\nFY24 incorporated two merged companies and FY25 two packaging proprietorships; acquired revenue is not separated. The 1 April 2022 appointed merger date differs from the 1 April 2023 accounting incorporation described in note59. Reported FY25/FY26 comparisons can be described, but cannot establish a clean three-year organic demand history. June product mix is heavily wedding jewellery, unlike FY26's regular-wear majority, so annualizing that quarter would create false confidence. Online order counts of 65,994/61,951/63,665 for FY24/FY25/FY26 do not substantiate the broad narrative of fast digital scaling. [S1 pp213, 303, 309, 333–339]\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nThe actual pipeline comprises an Ezra Street B2B showroom, further Rash Behari Avenue B2C floors and a Baruipur plating facility. It is not an unlimited national rollout. Management's ₹47.50/₹72.375 crore FY27/FY28 revenue assumptions are plans, not audited guidance achieved. The RHP contains inconsistent B2C descriptions: G+4, three-storied, ground/first floor and two/three additional selling floors. It also quotes ₹2.40 crore versus ₹1.60 crore of incremental B2C inventory in different explanations. The model uses the formal ₹5.60 crore total issue inventory object and does not manufacture a store-productivity forecast from those inconsistent descriptions. [S1 pp130–157]\\n\\nFour proposed PVD machines are specified at one kg/hour, seven productive hours/day, 288 working days/year: 8,064 kg/year. The ₹8.8037 crore funded plant cost excludes gifted land valued at ₹0.29 crore; ₹9.0937 crore is the stated full land-plus-project capital. It is not all machine cost: civil construction is ₹5.8302 crore and four machines ₹2.9735 crore. Completion is intended for October 2027, conditional on IPO realization and approvals. No observed plant utilization, sale price per kg or savings per kg exists. [S1 pp145–150]\\n\\nAt 50%/75%/100% utilization, the facility would process 4,032/6,048/8,064 kg annually. A 15% pretax cash return on ₹8.8037 crore alone would require about ₹3,275/₹2,183/₹1,638 incremental annual cash contribution per kg respectively, before considering additional working capital, taxes and sustaining capex. These are hurdle arithmetic, not demonstrated unit economics. FY26 outsourced plating expense of ₹2.5562 crore is a gross ceiling on replaceable expense at the old scale, not a free saving: in-house labour, consumables, energy, compliance, maintenance and depreciation remain. The model does not simply add that entire expense to profit. [S1 pp303, 332–339]\\n\\nIncremental inventory is working capital even though the prospectus describes initial stocking as capital expenditure. The model counts it once inside ONWC, not once as inventory and again as depreciable capex. Expansion quotes are inclusive of taxes; adding GST again would double-count the cash need. Later growth has additional fixed-capital and maintenance requirements. GQS cannot be strong when real demand, cohort returns and cash conversion remain unverified.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\nThe Qasim/Usman family controls the issuer. The post-IPO promoter/promoter-group position is approximately 72.26%; exact share counts, rather than inconsistent rounded RHP percentages, govern the share bridge. The 9 October exchange filing declares no outstanding warrants, convertibles, ESOPs or promoter encumbrances. All 11,249,563 pre-IPO shares are locked until specified 2027–2029 dates. Promoter/group holdings are 11,211,772 (72.26%); public holdings are 4,304,991 (27.74%). The immediately unlocked IPO float is 4,267,200 shares (27.50%), not the same as the public percentage. Free IPO shares do not guarantee ready exit. [S2]\\n\\nA 16:1 bonus in March 2026 changes share units, not economic ownership. The May 2025 2,223-share allotment settled a takeover liability at face value plus ₹262 premium, and must not be mistaken for cash proceeds. The October IPO is genuine fresh dilution: pre-issue ownership is spread over 15,516,763 shares instead of 11,249,563. The exact equity-growth and financing effects are separate from historical EPS. [S1 pp298, 303; S2]\\n\\nThe related-party loan from Mohammed Usman increased from ₹1.7032 crore in March to ₹3.0640 crore in June, interest free. That supported operations, but no irrevocable future funding or verified standstill is assumed. FY26 director remuneration disclosed in the RPT schedule is small relative to PAT; that does not eliminate larger capital-allocation issues. FY24 related advances and merged entities, MK Jewellers purchases and unmatched related-party balances deserve confirmation. No evidence reviewed substantiates diversion. [S1 pp304–305, 353]\\n\\n| Dated commitment / milestone | Verified position at cutoff | Treatment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Ground-floor B2C opening, April 2026 | RHP states operating; separate mature-store economics unavailable | Delivered operating milestone, not proof of return |\\n| IPO/listing, October 2026 | Official listing and shares verified | Completed capital-market milestone |\\n| Ezra showroom, February–March 2027 | MOU, definitive acquisition not executed at RHP date | Pending, not missed yet |\\n| B2C fit-out, March 2027 | Tax-inclusive budget and staged works; inconsistent floor descriptions | Pending; require final scope |\\n| Baruipur plating, October 2027 | Land available, approvals and construction/machinery execution outstanding | Pending; model includes delay/overrun |\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\nThe following values are ₹crore, converted from source ₹lakh. June is three months, not a full year. “Post-IPO equivalent EPS” divides historical profit by current shares for valuation comparability; it is not the historical accounting EPS. Other-income-adjusted reported PAT uses a disclosed 25% analytical tax on removed other income, not a claimed tax restatement; fully normalized core PAT is shown separately. Normalized operating EBIT removes all other income and adds finance cost; operating EBITDA then adds D&A. Source KPI EBITDA sometimes includes other income or omits the small finance add-back, hence the definition is rebuilt rather than copied. [S1 pp290–314]\\n\\n| ₹ crore, except EPS | FY24 | FY25 | FY26 | Apr–Jun 26 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Revenue | 13.2849 | 17.7719 | 30.3571 | 7.4928 |\\n| Jewellery | 8.9571 | 12.8184 | 24.8572 | 7.3586 |\\n| Cosmetics | 3.3567 | 4.4356 | 4.9026 | 0.0000 |\\n| Hotel | 0.9711 | 0.5179 | 0.5973 | 0.1342 |\\n| Other Income | 1.1725 | 0.0507 | 1.1846 | 0.0017 |\\n| Operating Ebitda | 0.1171 | 2.9779 | 7.3448 | 2.5551 |\\n| Operating Ebit | -0.1443 | 2.7500 | 7.1218 | 2.4759 |\\n| Pat | 0.9478 | 1.9972 | 6.2869 | 1.8286 |\\n| Other-income-adjusted reported PAT | 0.0684 | 1.9592 | 5.3984 | 1.8273 |\\n| Core PAT, no loss tax shield | -0.1476 | 2.0607 | 5.3341 | 1.8559 |\\n| CFO | -0.2610 | 0.0309 | -0.6778 | -0.3539 |\\n| Cash Capex | 1.1879 | 0.0032 | 0.3635 | 0.0467 |\\n| Reported CFO minus cash capex | -1.4489 | 0.0277 | -1.0413 | -0.4006 |\\n| Debt | 0.6090 | 0.6088 | 1.7032 | 3.0640 |\\n| Cash | 0.3406 | 0.1408 | 0.1291 | 0.2495 |\\n| Equity | 7.8177 | 9.8149 | 16.1622 | 17.9908 |\\n| Investment Property | 0.2910 | 0.3414 | 5.8563 | 6.6960 |\\n| Financial Investments | 3.9966 | 4.0242 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 |\\n| Trade receivables | 0.4850 | 1.5587 | 3.4193 | 5.6843 |\\n| Inventory | 1.0861 | 5.0931 | 7.8287 | 9.5581 |\\n| Trade payables | 1.8774 | 5.0330 | 3.8800 | 4.1745 |\\n| Finance-side capital proxy | 4.0895 | 6.2587 | 17.7363 | 20.8053 |\\n| Reported EPS on post-IPO shares | 0.6108 | 1.2871 | 4.0517 | 1.1785 |\\n\\n\\nThe series labeled normalized_pat_tax_assumed in the machine-readable table is only other-income-adjusted reported PAT, not fully normalized earnings: FY24 remains ₹0.0684 crore because the original deferred-tax benefit is retained. The separate fully_normalized_pat_no_loss_shield series charges 25% on positive core pretax profit and allows no tax shield for losses; FY24 is negative ₹0.1476 crore. Core EBIT is negative ₹0.1443 crore. This distinction prevents a false positive core-profit base.\\n\\n| Calculated metric | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Cumulative Cfo | -0.9079 |\\n| Cumulative Pat | 9.2319 |\\n| Cumulative Fcf Proxy | -2.4625 |\\n| Reported Two Year Cagr | 0.5116 |\\n| Cumulative Cfo Pat | -0.0983 |\\n| Marketcap | 106.4450 |\\n| Pe Reported | 16.9312 |\\n| Pe Normalized Tax Assumed | 19.7177 |\\n| Historical Ev Stale Not Postipo | 108.0191 |\\n| Ipo Gross | 34.9910 |\\n| Postipo Dilution Existing Fraction | 0.2750 |\\n| June Finance Capital Proxy | 20.8053 |\\n| June Operating Asset Bridge | 21.6091 |\\n| June Tax Liabilities Bridge | 0.8038 |\\n| June Bridge Rounding Difference | 0.0000 |\\n| Fy25 Top10 Recalculated Pct | 23.2046 |\\n| June Aged Over 6M Pct | 17.9019 |\\n\\n\\nThe ratio of three-year summed CFO to summed PAT is negative; it is not the average of annual conversion ratios. FY26 pre-working-capital cash profit of ₹7.3396 crore was reduced by receivables, inventory, lower trade credit and advances, before cash taxes, to negative CFO. Capex is an additional investing use and cannot explain away weak operating cash. Cash capex FCF shown above is only a reported proxy; investment-property spending is separately embedded in the investment section and increases the broader reinvestment burden. FY24's noncash-looking merger lines prevent an unqualified reconstructed total-cash-capex series. [S1 pp44–47, 292]\\n\\nThe finance-side capital proxy is equity plus borrowings less cash and genuinely financial investments, keeping investment property inside the business. June gross operating assets less operating liabilities are ₹21.6091 crore; deferred/current tax liabilities of ₹0.8038 crore bridge to ₹20.8053 crore finance-side capital, subject to disclosure rounding. This is not identical to precisely normalized operating IC because supplier/government advances, property classification, tax balances and acquired entities require further reconciliation. FY25–FY26 normalized NOPAT changes and capital changes are reproducibly calculated, but are not sufficient to score CAP as a verified project ROIC. Average-capital returns and incremental returns have different denominators and are not confused.\\n\\n| Period | Δ NOPAT | Δ IC proxy | Incremental return | Average IC | NOPAT/avg IC | DSO | DIO | DPO proxy | CCC proxy |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY25 | 2.21 | 2.17 | 101.73% | 5.17 | 39.86% | 21.0 | 88.9 | 99.5 | 10.5 |\\n| FY26 | 3.28 | 11.48 | 28.57% | 12.00 | 44.52% | 29.9 | 140.1 | 96.7 | 73.4 |\\n\\n\\nDPO uses average trade payables divided by materials plus traded purchases plus inventory change, a compatible-cost proxy rather than verified credit purchases. DSO uses revenue; DIO uses the same cost proxy. CCC = DSO + DIO − DPO. Acquisition scope, property and noncash merger balances limit comparability. Minor ₹0.01 lakh differences between balance-sheet and ageing AP totals are treated as disclosure rounding; the ageing totals are used consistently.\\n\\nReceivables over six months increase from approximately ₹0.0236 crore in March to ₹1.0176 crore in June, including ₹0.1330 crore aged one to two years, with no doubtful allowance shown. Total inventory reaches ₹9.5581 crore, of which finished goods ₹6.6869 crore. The company's reported inventory days rise 38 → 85 → 117 → 205; receivable days 16 → 21 → 30 → 55. Some published cycle figures disagree (167 versus 179 days for June). Ratios computed from average balances and material-cost denominators are separately labeled; they are not substituted for the company's differing holding-period definitions. [S1 pp134–138, 300, 310–311]\\n\\nFull audit and disclosure reconciliation register:\\n\\n**A01 · S1 pp287-289**\\n\\nRead complete restatement examination report of Murarka & Associates, dated24 September2026. It says no qualifications requiring restatement adjustments. It explicitly is not a reissued original audit opinion. Original statutory reports, CARO and IFC annexures FY24-FY26 were not located in issuer investor index or public primary searches. Original-audit completeness remains ND, not clean assurance.\\n\\n**A02 · S1 pp292,298,303,309**\\n\\nCFS FY24 has a 2023 column header despite matching FY24 amounts. FY24 includes merger fixed assets−207.09lakh, merger reserves/P&L19.07lakh, deferred-tax−1.02lakh, share-capital33.35lakh and premium342.65lakh. Notes identify share-exchange amalgamation, not cash financing. FY26 CFS includes6.04lakh capital/premium although note3 describes2223 shares against a604993rupee takeover liability. Do not infer these lines are cash inflows/outflows. Preserve reported cash bridges but original bank-level and noncash reconciliation required; cannot safely restate only one leg.\\n\\n**A03 · S1 pp298,303,309**\\n\\nMerger shares differ between note3/30 (249128 plus84388) and note59 (270200 plus84377). Official post-IPO issued count is15516763 and controls current valuation. Historical merger economics and complete historic diluted-share bridge unresolved; bonus16:1 is not economic dilution.\\n\\n**A04 · S1 pp299-300,307-310**\\n\\nNoncurrent investments669.60lakh at June are investment in property. Cash24.95lakh includes15.78lakh cash in hand and9.17lakh bank. Receivables568.43lakh include88.46lakh aged6-12months and13.30lakh aged1-2years; no doubtful allowance. Note51 says receivables/payables/advances subject to confirmation. Do not net property against debt or use it for monthly cash rescue. Collection deterioration matters even with unmodified restatement wording.\\n\\n**A05 · S1 pp304-305,307**\\n\\nRPT loan from Mohammed Usman rose170.32 to306.40lakh in Q1; interest free. FY26 related MK Jewellers purchases72.06lakh, closing36.88lakh; June purchases9.13lakh, closing29.98lakh. FY24 Shaistah Usman advance314lakh both advanced/repaid. MD Aurangzeb advance5.75lakh in RPT differs from aggregate related-advance1.70lakh at June. No diversion proved. Need counterparty-confirmed reconciliation and contractual repayment/standstill terms. Historical gross RPT flows are not annual expense totals.\\n\\n**A06 · S1 pp307,309 and355-357**\\n\\nDetailed litigation table at printed357 totals34.19lakh for three tax matters; note33 components32.95+1.23 total34.18lakh, a0.01lakh rounding difference; older summary33.23lakh is stale. No criminal/regulatory promoter/company proceedings disclosed. Note43 says no outstanding loans but note5 and debt chapter show related-party debt. Use0.3419crore in linked tax-payment stress. Wording conflicts require reconciliation, not allegation.\\n\\n**A07 · S1 pp40-43,198-215,333-339**\\n\\nFY25 top10 customer412.39lakh divided by1777.19lakh equals23.20%, not20.24% printed. Customer tenure narrative4.77years differs table6.6. June segment/channel denominators differ. MD&A June PAT188.86lakh conflicts financial182.86lakh. Use financial statements and distinguish inconsistent operating tables; no false precision in organic or concentration scoring.\\n\\n**A08 · S1 pp293-296,298-314**\\n\\nAll financial notes1-61 and receivable/payable ageing and AS17 segment annexures were reviewed. Indian GAAP AS/DivisionI; no subsidiaries; inventories management-certified; finished goods lower cost/NRV, RM/WIP at cost. No bank facilities, no declared dividends. Restated financial extraction is Medium confidence, financial reliability/growth/valuation conclusions Low pending original-audit and scope reconciliation.\\n\\n**A09 · S1 notes4 and9, printed298 and306**\\n\\nNote9 states58.58lakh previously uncharged depreciation was adjusted against reserves; note4 records86.09lakh prior-period expense. These are distinct from the examination report statement about audit qualifications. Original audits and period-by-period restatement schedules are needed to establish which originating periods bore these charges. No claim of complete clean original-audit reconciliation is made.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\nThe market capitalization is price times the verified current issued share count. Reported and normalized FY26 P/E are shown above. Do not use ₹5.59 historical EPS with the expanded post-IPO share count. Do not describe the business as debt-free merely because there is no bank borrowing. A defensible current EV, current P/B and current FCF yield are NS: the old June cash/debt/balance sheet predates the IPO and substantial deployment. A stale EV can be calculated for illustration but is not presented as a live valuation multiple.\\n\\nThe model is deliberately conditional. Starting at observed June cash, it rolls monthly assumptions through the assumed 9 October settlement and the entry date without resetting opening cash. IPO gross proceeds of ₹34.99104 crore are verified by issue shares × ₹82; the RHP does not finalize expenses and net general-purpose cash. The central fee assumption is ₹3 crore, tested at ₹2/₹4.5 crore. The separate object pools are ₹16.6537 crore fixed projects, ₹5.60 crore opening inventory and ₹5.2144 crore working capital; the residual after fees is general cash. These pools are analytically earmarked, not freely available to pay any obligation. No change of objects is assumed. No property is treated as cash.\\n\\nPre-entry timing assumes 60% of modeled monthly net operating cash precedes month-end requirements and 40% follows. Post-entry monthly requirements are conservatively paid before that month's operating cash. The full event ledger records both stages, cash floors and debt repayments. These timing assumptions must be replaced by actual bank and deployment records before R is scoreable. The base does not simply copy management's 50%-plus growth targets: revenue steps to ₹40/48/56/64/70 crore with EBITDA margins 18%/19% thereafter. The bear has demand loss, lower margins, more ONWC, a one-year plant delay and 25% cost overrun; the bull requires higher sales and maintained margins. [Model assumptions A]\\n\\nThe primary conditional terminal enterprise value is next-year FCFF divided by the 15% discount rate less 4% growth. This ties terminal value to paid-for capital and earned cash returns, rather than assuming that a market multiple is deserved. The implied EBITDA multiple is an output, not an input. Cash above both the operating floor and liquidity reserve is added, debt is deducted, and fully diluted future shares determine per-share value.\\n\\n| Case | Optional rescue | Year-5 sales | Earned EV | Implied EBITDA multiple | Earned value/share | Funded conditional CAGR | First breach | New shares |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | False | 36.00 | 12.47 | 2.89 | 10.22 | NS | 2026-07-31 preentry_requirements | 0 |\\n| bear | True | 36.00 | 12.47 | 2.89 | 10.22 | NS | 2026-07-31 preentry_requirements | 0 |\\n| base | False | 70.00 | 61.46 | 4.62 | 55.75 | -4.06 | None | 0 |\\n| base | True | 70.00 | 61.46 | 4.62 | 55.75 | -4.06 | None | 0 |\\n| bull | False | 112.00 | 122.57 | 4.76 | 78.99 | NS | 2031-10-19 terminal_capital_catchup | 0 |\\n| bull | True | 112.00 | 122.57 | 4.76 | 74.81 | 1.75 | None | 868218 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | False | 70.00 | 61.95 | 4.66 | 54.46 | -4.51 | None | 0 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | True | 70.00 | 61.95 | 4.66 | 54.46 | -4.51 | None | 0 |\\n\\n\\nA value labeled “terminal arithmetic” after a breach is not a realizable price target or ordinary investor return. The ordinary return and hurdle entry are NS after the first unfinanced cash-floor breach, even if subsequent counterfactual profits would refill cash. This prevents a false high return from a business that could not pay its earlier obligations. The optional equity branch is explicitly hypothetical: price ₹54.88, 3% fees, at most ₹8 crore gross and at most 1,483,237 new whole shares, also constrained by the current 17,000,000-share authorized ceiling. At the terminal capital event, any hypothetical rescue also prefunds the following month’s reinvestment and maintenance requirement so that raising merely to the cash floor cannot create a false funded terminal state. It cannot cure a pre-entry breach and it is not committed financing. Issuance approvals and buyers are not inferred from legal headroom.\\n\\n| Case, no rescue | Year | Sales | NOPAT | CFO | Capex | ΔONWC | Closing cash | Closing debt | Closing fixed | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | 1 | 30.00 | 1.81 | -5.31 | 0.75 | 5.60 | -0.15 | 0.00 | 11.81 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| bear | 2 | 28.00 | 1.19 | 2.12 | 14.58 | 0.00 | 1.27 | 0.00 | 25.45 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| bear | 3 | 30.00 | 1.33 | 2.86 | 7.89 | 0.00 | -0.99 | 0.00 | 31.81 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| bear | 4 | 33.00 | 1.78 | 3.37 | 1.12 | 0.00 | 1.25 | 0.00 | 31.36 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| bear | 5 | 36.00 | 2.07 | 3.63 | 1.20 | 0.00 | 3.68 | 0.00 | 30.99 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| base | 1 | 40.00 | 4.77 | -0.54 | 11.90 | 5.60 | 9.39 | 3.06 | 22.34 | conditional_funded |\\n| base | 2 | 48.00 | 5.85 | 7.17 | 6.51 | 0.00 | 12.54 | 0.00 | 27.53 | conditional_funded |\\n| base | 3 | 56.00 | 6.94 | 8.33 | 1.76 | 0.00 | 19.10 | 0.00 | 27.90 | conditional_funded |\\n| base | 4 | 64.00 | 8.06 | 6.93 | 1.92 | 2.55 | 26.66 | 0.00 | 28.41 | conditional_funded |\\n| base | 5 | 70.00 | 8.90 | 0.71 | 3.81 | 9.62 | 25.48 | 0.00 | 30.78 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 1 | 47.50 | 7.20 | 1.90 | 12.05 | 5.60 | 12.19 | 3.06 | 22.49 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 2 | 65.00 | 10.21 | 11.10 | 6.85 | 0.45 | 19.37 | 0.00 | 28.00 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 3 | 81.00 | 12.89 | 9.85 | 2.90 | 4.48 | 30.81 | 0.00 | 29.46 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 4 | 97.00 | 15.59 | 12.63 | 3.22 | 4.48 | 40.41 | 0.00 | 31.16 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 5 | 112.00 | 18.11 | -28.35 | 15.98 | 48.08 | -3.92 | 0.00 | 45.53 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 1 | 40.00 | 4.77 | -3.36 | 11.90 | 6.87 | 7.84 | 3.06 | 22.34 | conditional_funded |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 2 | 48.00 | 5.85 | 3.33 | 6.51 | 3.84 | 10.98 | 0.00 | 27.53 | conditional_funded |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 3 | 56.00 | 6.94 | 4.49 | 1.76 | 3.84 | 13.71 | 0.00 | 27.90 | conditional_funded |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 4 | 64.00 | 8.06 | 5.63 | 1.92 | 3.84 | 17.42 | 0.00 | 28.41 | conditional_funded |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 5 | 70.00 | 8.90 | 7.45 | 1.88 | 2.88 | 22.99 | 0.00 | 28.86 | conditional_funded |\\n\\n\\nThe following 5×/8×/10× EBITDA exits are secondary assumed market-multiple sensitivities, not the central earned-value forecast. Any funded CAGR here is a conditional market-price outcome, not the primary cash-earned result.\\n\\n| Case | Optional rescue | Year-5 sales | Year-5 EBITDA | Minimum cash | First breach | New shares | Terminal arithmetic/share | Funded conditional CAGR | 15% hurdle entry |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | False | 36.00 | 4.32 | -2.19 | 2026-07-31 preentry_requirements | 0 | 16.10 | NS% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 36.00 | 4.32 | -2.19 | 2026-07-31 preentry_requirements | 0 | 16.10 | NS% | NS |\\n| base | False | 70.00 | 13.30 | 0.13 | None | 0 | 84.71 | 4.31% | 42.12 |\\n| base | True | 70.00 | 13.30 | 0.13 | None | 0 | 84.71 | 4.31% | 42.12 |\\n| bull | False | 112.00 | 25.76 | -3.92 | 2031-10-19 terminal_capital_catchup | 0 | 166.01 | NS% | NS |\\n| bull | True | 112.00 | 25.76 | 0.24 | None | 868218 | 157.22 | 18.04% | 78.16 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | False | 70.00 | 13.30 | 0.13 | None | 0 | 83.11 | 3.91% | 41.32 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | True | 70.00 | 13.30 | 0.13 | None | 0 | 83.11 | 3.91% | 41.32 |\\n\\n\\nTerminal EV/EBITDA is an equity-return cross-check, not a forecast that the market will award that multiple. Debt is subtracted, only unrestricted excess cash above the floor and next-month operating-liquidity reserve is added, and operating property is not added a second time. There are no dividends, so retained cash is not counted again as distributions. Five-year total returns exclude investor taxes and transaction/market-impact costs. A 5% exit haircut would reduce annualized gross wealth return by a factor of 0.95^(1/5), roughly 1.02% of the gross annual wealth factor; actual impact may be much larger in one-sided SME trading.\\n\\nA second, FCFF valuation discounts monthly-aggregated annual NOPAT + D&A − cash capex − ΔONWC. Terminal growth is 4%, below each 12%/15%/18% discount rate. Next-year reinvestment is the greater of sales-based ONWC/fixed growth and NOPAT × growth / 15% terminal ROIC. Before valuation, missing opening capital needed to earn that ROIC on next-year average capital is paid from unrestricted cash, with depreciation feedback solved algebraically; excess existing capital is never released for free. Maintenance equals terminal D&A only as an explicit steady-state assumption, not a historical fact. A twelve-month continuation tests capital and maintenance payments before operating receipts, without adding those future cash receipts to year-five equity. Terminal capital, cash, debt and shares are preserved in the canonical record. Discount rates are analytical, not a claimed empirically measured WACC; the 15% equity hurdle is a separate assumption.\\n\\n| Case | Discount | EV | Terminal PV share, % | Per-share equity | Validity |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | 0.12 | -6.38 | -152.45 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| bear | 0.15 | -9.30 | -66.67 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| bear | 0.18 | -10.64 | -40.27 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| base | 0.12 | 43.47 | 110.32 | 43.56 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| base | 0.15 | 25.88 | 118.09 | 31.63 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| base | 0.18 | 16.26 | 129.80 | 24.89 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| bull | 0.12 | 75.72 | 126.29 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| bull | 0.15 | 43.23 | 140.96 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| bull | 0.18 | 26.25 | 160.37 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 0.12 | 39.63 | 121.95 | 42.04 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 0.15 | 21.81 | 141.23 | 30.03 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 0.18 | 12.07 | 176.31 | 23.27 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n\\n\\nPrimary earned-value hurdle entry prices:\\n\\n| Case | Rescue | 12% entry | 15% entry | 18% entry |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | False | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bear | True | NS | NS | NS |\\n| base | False | 31.64 | 27.72 | 24.37 |\\n| base | True | 31.64 | 27.72 | 24.37 |\\n| bull | False | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bull | True | 42.45 | 37.19 | 32.70 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | False | 30.90 | 27.08 | 23.81 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | True | 30.90 | 27.08 | 23.81 |\\n\\n\\n| Case | Rescue | Capital before | Catch-up paid | Capital after | Next NOPAT | Next reinvestment | Earned ROIC | Continuation breach |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | False | 48.64 | 0.00 | 48.64 | 2.21 | 0.84 | 4.50 | None |\\n| bear | True | 48.64 | 0.00 | 48.64 | 2.21 | 0.84 | 4.50 | None |\\n| base | False | 50.61 | 9.63 | 60.24 | 9.22 | 2.46 | 15.00 | None |\\n| base | True | 50.61 | 9.63 | 60.24 | 9.22 | 2.46 | 15.00 | None |\\n| bull | False | 63.70 | 56.41 | 120.12 | 18.39 | 4.90 | 15.00 | 2032 terminal month1 requirements |\\n| bull | True | 63.70 | 56.41 | 120.12 | 18.39 | 4.90 | 15.00 | None |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | False | 61.81 | 0.00 | 61.81 | 9.29 | 2.48 | 14.74 | None |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | True | 61.81 | 0.00 | 61.81 | 9.29 | 2.48 | 14.74 | None |\\n\\n\\nTerminal growth/discount cross-sensitivity, rerunning capital and funding at every growth assumption:\\n\\n| Terminal growth | Discount | Cash breach | Per current share |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 0.02 | 0.12 | None | 41.50 |\\n| 0.02 | 0.15 | None | 31.43 |\\n| 0.02 | 0.18 | None | 25.25 |\\n| 0.04 | 0.12 | None | 43.56 |\\n| 0.04 | 0.15 | None | 31.63 |\\n| 0.04 | 0.18 | None | 24.89 |\\n| 0.06 | 0.12 | None | 46.84 |\\n| 0.06 | 0.15 | None | 31.84 |\\n| 0.06 | 0.18 | None | 24.37 |\\n\\n\\nDCF values are conditional cross-checks, not a way around a cash breach. The DCF equity bridge adds entry unrestricted cash plus only the discounted value of actual eligible future pool draws, matched to the same annual timing used for FCFF, then deducts entry debt. It does not add the full opening earmark at par. Undrawn restricted cash has no credited value: in the base, ₹0.6339 crore remains restricted at the horizon and is not treated as distributable or freely repurposable. This is deliberately conservative rather than assuming a change of objects. Per-current-share DCF is suppressed for all rescue-enabled branches rather than ignoring future financiers’ claims; it is also suppressed after a funding breach. A high terminal-value share demonstrates sensitivity, not confidence.\\n\\nTwo-way value sensitivity, holding the base operating path except the stated dimensions:\\n\\n| Margin shift | Terminal multiple | Cash breach | Funded CAGR | Hurdle entry |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| -.03 | 6 | None | -2.11 | 30.65 |\\n| -.03 | 8 | None | 2.09 | 37.83 |\\n| -.03 | 10 | None | 5.70 | 45.01 |\\n| 0 | 6 | None | -0.30 | 33.59 |\\n| 0 | 8 | None | 4.31 | 42.12 |\\n| 0 | 10 | None | 8.23 | 50.64 |\\n| .03 | 6 | None | 1.28 | 36.35 |\\n| .03 | 8 | None | 6.27 | 46.22 |\\n| .03 | 10 | None | 10.46 | 56.09 |\\n\\n\\nWorking-capital and issue-cost sensitivity, with complete monthly cash paths retained:\\n\\n| Extra ONWC/sales | IPO fees, ₹ crore | Cash breach | Min.cash | Funded CAGR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 0 | 2 | None | 0.13 | 4.47 |\\n| 0 | 3 | None | 0.13 | 4.31 |\\n| 0 | 4.5 | None | 0.13 | 4.07 |\\n| .08 | 2 | None | 0.13 | 4.52 |\\n| .08 | 3 | None | 0.13 | 4.36 |\\n| .08 | 4.5 | None | 0.13 | 4.13 |\\n| .16 | 2 | None | 0.13 | 4.32 |\\n| .16 | 3 | None | 0.13 | 4.16 |\\n| .16 | 4.5 | None | 0.13 | 3.92 |\\n\\n\\nThe June current-tax provision of ₹0.5518 crore is paid in projection month one separately from forward tax. Deferred tax of ₹0.2520 crore is not a cash payable. Bear and the separate base collection/funding stress pay ₹0.3419 crore for the detailed litigation-table tax contingency and absorb ₹1.21021 crore of collection/stock-replacement cash loss in month three (25% of June receivables older than six months plus 10% of June inventory). The latter is an analytical loss-and-replenishment stress, not an allegation or a booked impairment. The stress raises ONWC intensity by 16 percentage points and runs both no-rescue and bounded-rescue paths. The 18-month project-payment schedule is an analyst reschedule after entry, not the issuer’s fiscal-year object-utilization table.\\n\\nThe reverse test solves the constant revenue growth needed for price preservation or 12%/15%/18% annual equity hurdles, under base margins and capital intensity. The “earned” rows use the primary FCFF perpetuity; 6×/8×/10× rows are secondary market-multiple comparisons. A feasible solution must also survive every modeled cash event without rescue. It tests a one-percentage-point grid from 0–60% annual growth, refining valid local brackets. No-solution results apply to that declared search, not a proof that no mathematical solution exists anywhere. Every grid and bisection candidate has a complete deduplicated cash path in the canonical record. A funded threshold adjacent to an unfunded candidate is explicitly a funding boundary, not a continuous equality root.\\n\\n| Terminal multiple | Annual hurdle | Required revenue CAGR | Year-5 sales | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| earned | 0 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| earned | 12.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| earned | 15.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| earned | 18.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 6 | 0 | 20.40 | 76.81 | Conditional funded equality bracket; not demand/capacity validation |\\n| 6 | 12.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 6 | 15.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 6 | 18.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 8 | 0 | 11.39 | 52.07 | Conditional funded equality bracket; not demand/capacity validation |\\n| 8 | 12.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 8 | 15.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 8 | 18.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 10 | 0 | 7.19 | 42.95 | Conditional funded equality bracket; not demand/capacity validation |\\n| 10 | 12.00 | 25.43 | 94.24 | Conditional funded equality bracket; not demand/capacity validation |\\n| 10 | 15.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 10 | 18.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n\\n\\nThese solutions establish mathematical expectations, not verified accessible demand or plant capacity. No kg-to-revenue translation is claimed without product weight/realization data. The conditional hurdle-entry numbers can help frame price discipline but are not an actionable fair-value range while G2/G3/G4 remain unresolved.\\n\\nLiquidity is a separate risk. There are six possible post-listing sessions through 19 October and actual issuer trade rows on 12, 13 and 19 October. Absent rows on other dates are retained as absent/ND rather than silently invented zero records; pre-listing dates are NA. The 19 October trade is one 1,600-share lot with ₹109,760 turnover. Four lots cost ₹439,040; five cost ₹548,800, so a ₹5 lakh position cannot be matched exactly. Observed-only mean/median and 2%/5%/10% arithmetic are stored as incomplete illustrations, not the required 60-session liquidation test. Bid/ask spread and executable depth are ND. Therefore L and IAS remain NS. [S2–S4]\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe decisive failure variable is conversion of fashionable, increasingly aged inventory and credit sales into unrestricted cash. A regional demand or fashion reversal can reduce sales while finished goods need markdowns; receivables age, suppliers require quicker payment, and the plant absorbs cash before delivering savings. Promoter repayment or missing bank access can make the timing fatal even with positive year-end earnings. An IPO does not make every future rupee spendable for every purpose. The shareholder can suffer economic loss through lower durable earnings, forced dilution, distressed disposal or inability to exit, not merely a temporary quotation decline.\\n\\nImpairment risk is High on this evidence. The contrary case is real: IPO proceeds, reported jewellery growth, modest top-customer concentration and property assets could allow a successful transition. But property realizability, title documents, net proceeds after sale, lease commitments and a liquid market have not been independently valued. No liquidation floor is invented. A hypothetical funding failure is not described as actual insolvency or misconduct.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\nA fivefold price in five years requires approximately 38% annual price growth; tenfold requires 58.5%. At unchanged shares, ₹68.60 becoming ₹343/₹686 requires equity values of roughly ₹532/₹1,064 crore before interim distributions. At 10× terminal EBITDA and ignoring a favorable net-cash offset, that means about ₹53/₹106 crore EBITDA, far beyond the present ₹7.34 crore operating EBITDA base. A 20% margin would imply approximately ₹266/₹532 crore sales, plus the matching inventory, fixed capital and dilution. No verified unit economics, accessible customer demand or funded store footprint supports that outcome. The fully linked scale tests below do not claim these sales are accessible. They apply constant growth to the approximate ₹266/₹532 crore scale, 19% first-year/20% later EBITDA margins and the same capital/funding rules, with and without bounded rescue. They do not convert an unfunded arithmetic target into an ordinary return.\\n\\n| Illustrative target | Sales CAGR | Year-5 sales | Rescue | First breach | New shares | Earned funded CAGR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 5× | 54.36 | 266 | False | 2031-10-19 terminal_capital_catchup | 0 | NS |\\n| 5× | 54.36 | 266 | True | 2031-10-19 terminal_capital_catchup | 1457725 | NS |\\n| 10× | 77.31 | 532 | False | 2030-01-19 requirements_before_receipts | 0 | NS |\\n| 10× | 77.31 | 532 | True | 2030-08-19 requirements_before_receipts | 1457725 | NS |\\n\\n\\nClassification: Speculative / unsupported. A lower terminal multiple or new shares makes the hurdle harder, not easier.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\nThese are research review triggers, not scheduled automations and not automatic trading rules.\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Warning / thesis-breaking evidence | Review source |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Audits and cash reconciliation | Restatement report only; noncash-looking merger/takeover cash lines | Unreconciled bank flows or scope limitation; material misstatement would overturn reliability | Original FY24–FY26 reports, CARO/IFC and bank reconciliations |\\n| Collections | June receivables ₹5.6843 crore; over-six-month ₹1.0176 crore | Further ageing without cash recovery; material write-off versus equity | Next interim/annual ageing and subsequent collections |\\n| Inventory productivity | June ₹9.5581 crore, finished goods ₹6.6869 crore | Stock rises faster than cash sales; markdowns eliminate presumed price/mix margin | Stock ageing, NRV policy and store-level sell-through |\\n| IPO cash and deployment | Gross ₹34.99104 crore; formal objects ₹27.4681 crore | Missing/changed uses, unavailable funds or unexplained RPT payments | Actual bank cash, issue expenses, utilization certificate |\\n| Related-party funding | June promoter loan ₹3.064 crore | Demand for repayment without demonstrated liquidity; new advance inconsistency | Agreements, confirmations and next balance sheet |\\n| B2B/B2C projects | Ezra target Feb–Mar27; B2C Mar27 | Definitive acquisition/approved scope delayed; no mature contribution despite working capital | Registered documents, fit-out completion and cohort economics |\\n| Plating | October27 target; 8,064 kg design capacity | Delay, overruns or low contribution per kg; returns below capital cost | Licences, commissioning, utilization and cost bridge |\\n| Organic demand | No verified volume/same-store bridge | Growth driven only by prices/acquired turnover or credit extension | Volumes, realization and customer cohorts |\\n| Tradability | ST; 1,600 lot; three actual trade rows | Repeated one-lot/one-price days, no executable bids | Official 20/60-session data and verified order-book conditions |\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\nAction category: research hold / pending evidence, not eligible for a numerical business, growth or investment ranking. This is not “avoid for proven governance abuse,” and it is not a verified current funding default. It fails the evidence-backed compounder designation because the requisite quality/growth evidence and several critical factors are not scoreable. No price alone cures missing audit, funding or execution-liquidity evidence. Once those are resolved, normalized margins, funded inventory needs and the actual entry cash balance should drive a fresh IAS. Neither the IPO price nor the original purchase cost, if any, determines intrinsic value.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n- S1: [R.K. Fashion Accessories RHP](https://affinityglobalcap.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/RHP_LFINAL-1.pdf), 2026-09-29. Printed287-314 financial report and all notes;129-161 objects;39-72 risks;197-227 business;237-282 governance;318-353 MD&A and debt\\n- S2: [NSE listing circular CML76831 and post-issue shareholding](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/circulars/CML76831.zip), 2026-10-09. \\n- S3: [NSE UDiFF market data](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip), 2026-10-19. \\n- S4: [NSE security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz), 2026-10-19. \\n- S5: [Issuer investor and RHP index](https://citygirljewellery.co.in/rhpl), 2026-10-23 retrieval. \\n\\n- P1: [Banaras Beads official company overview](https://www.banarasbead.com/about-us/) and the issuer RHP's peer comparison. Structural comparison only; no live peer multiple is used.\\n- P2: [Vaibhav Global official history and business overview](https://www.vaibhavglobal.com/public/vgl-at-a-glance). Structural comparison only; undated current site text is not evidence of a specific historical valuation.\\n- P3: [Goldiam May 2025 investor presentation](https://www.goldiam.com/pdf/corporate-announcement/2025-26/Investors%20Presentation%2031-3-2025.pdf), business and consignment inventory discussion. Older dated comparison, not current target multiple.\\n\\nV denotes an observed primary disclosure; C a reproducible calculation; M a management statement; A an analyst assumption. Historical data and the security master are V, ratio/score/model arithmetic C, capacity targets and rollout milestones M, and all future cash timing, margins, fees, capital intensity, repayment timing, rescue terms and valuation multiples A. Material source locators are attached to the relevant paragraphs and input records. Source-file hashes authenticate the retrieved vintage; they do not validate management's underlying claims.\\n\\nOpen questions, in order: original statutory audit and annexures; exact noncash/cash financing reconciliation; related-party and merger-share conflicts; final prospectus and IPO expense/net-cash/deployment proof; promoter-loan repayment terms; acquired/price/volume revenue bridge; store-unit and per-kg plating economics; customer ageing recoveries; original inventory ageing/NRV support; and sufficient verified trading history. No public claim that the full original-audit history was read is made. Financial tables preserve source rounding rather than forcing contradictory tables to agree.\\n\\nThe compact and full reconstruction packages contain authored inputs, the report, canonical data and deterministic scripts. All scenario, monthly cash, sensitivity and every tested reverse-expectation row are represented in canonical data. The full package adds convenient redundant tables. Neither contains original PDFs, OCR, source-page images, private service identifiers, credentials or transport helpers. Rebuild instructions delete both outputs and the validation attestation; successful fresh-download reconstruction establishes artifact completeness and arithmetic, not investment truth.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\nR.K. Fashion is a regional imitation-jewellery design, outsourcing and wholesale-distribution business, with cosmetics resale and hotel rental income. It is not yet an established owner-operated jewellery manufacturer: the first proposed in-house plating plant remains an execution project. Revenue and operating profit expanded sharply, but the strongest counterweight is cash: cumulative FY24–FY26 operating cash flow is negative despite positive reported profits in every year. Inventory, customer credit, property spending and promoter funding have grown together. At ₹68.60, the headline P/E understates the normalized P/E, while obscuring post-IPO capital risks. Classification: Hold — unresolved financial reliability, funding and tradability. BQS, GQS and IAS remain NS, with mathematical bounds rather than invented point scores. This is a speculative expansion/special-situation thesis, not an evidence-backed compounder. Reconciled original audits, verified IPO deployment and bank cash, collections and credible showroom/plant economics would be needed to change that conclusion. [S1, S2; A01–A09]\\n\\nThe conditional earned-value base produces a five-year CAGR of -4.06%; the bull requires hypothetical rescue and produces 1.75%. Those are analytical conditionals, not supported forecasts. Higher market-multiple outcomes shown later must not be mistaken for cash-earned intrinsic value.\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\nThe legal issuer was incorporated as Satabadi Distributor Private Limited in March 2010, renamed in February 2011 and converted to a public company in March 2026. The company has no holding company, subsidiaries or associates in the financial note reviewed. The report therefore uses standalone Indian GAAP Accounting Standards and Schedule III Division I, not an assumed Ind AS perimeter. FY24 includes merged businesses; FY25 includes two packaging proprietorship takeovers. The last audited/restated financial observation is 30 June 2026, not the valuation date. There is no comparable prior-June period from which to construct a defensible TTM. [S1 pp237–242, 290–314]\\n\\nNSE's 9 October listing circular and post-issue shareholding verify 15,516,763 issued fully paid shares, ₹82 issue price, listing from 12 October, ST trade-for-trade series and a 1,600-share trading lot. IPO application minimums are not confused with the secondary-market trading lot. The shares offered were 4,267,200, all fresh issue; no OFS. The five-year model runs from 19 October 2026 to 19 October 2031. The central equity hurdle is an analytical 15% nominal annual return before investor taxes; 12% and 18% are shown alongside it. ₹5 lakh is only the framework's illustrative liquidity reference, not a suitable allocation recommendation. [S2–S4]\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | Conditional | No substantiated diversion or minority abuse found; RPT, governance history and financial inconsistencies require reconciliation. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Original statutory/CARO/IFC unavailable, noncash merger and takeover lines in cash flow, conflicting merger/counterparty numbers; no reliable current investment conclusion. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | Actual entry cash/net proceeds/deployment/promoter-loan terms unverified. Conditional stress cash shortfall is not evidence of actual default. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | Six possible listed sessions and only three trade rows; 20/60-day implementation test unavailable, ST and 1,600-lot restrictions. |\\n\\n\\nConfidence is High for the current security identity, official close and post-issue share count; Medium for transcribed restated P&L, balance-sheet and cash-flow values; Low for organic growth, matched incremental returns, current cash and investment attractiveness. Overall confidence is Low because the unresolved areas are material. The financial examiner's statement about no qualifications requiring adjustments is not a substitute for the original statutory audit, CARO or internal-financial-control reports.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\nNS means not scoreable, not zero or an average score. Known factors retain their original weights. Bounds below are mathematical missing-data bounds, not confidence intervals. No score is rank-eligible while critical evidence and gates remain unresolved. Decimal arithmetic is retained before one-decimal half-up display. The exact original weights and their contributions are reproduced below.\\n\\n| Measure | Known weighted sum | Coverage | Raw/final | Raw mathematical bound | Effective bound |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | 2.70 | 68.00% | NS | 3.02–5.90 | 3.02–5.90 |\\n| GQS | 1.80 | 45.00% | NS | 2.35–7.30 | 2.35–5.00 |\\n| IAS | 0.90 | 30.00% | NS | 1.60–7.90 | 1.60–7.90 |\\n\\n\\nCASH = 2 reflects persistent negative multi-year realization, not simply one quarter of showroom stocking. It triggers the framework's GQS ceiling of 5. Because GQS is already NS, the result remains NS, not a point score of 5. There is no governance override: adverse evidence is not sufficient to allege serious abuse or assign GOV ≤3. There is no actual funding-failure override: hypothetical cash breaches do not prove the issuer defaulted. IAS is NS independently because BQS, R and L are not scoreable.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence and contrary fact |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | No OFS; IPO all primary capital and no pledge/convertibles in 9 Oct shareholding [S2]. Family-related funding and transactions are disclosed; no auditor change in preceding 3 years [S1 p 88]. Contrary: noncash financing and related balances conflict; newly constituted independent oversight has no long listed record [A02,A03,A05]. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | Regional wholesale relationships and branded design/quality coordination exist [S1 pp 197-227]. Top 10 FY26 customers 15.15%; no exclusive Karigar/noncompete and no committed order book [S1 pp 40-49]. Contrary: 12-15% pricing action and strong jewellery revenue growth show some commercial franchise; durable excess returns and switching costs are unverified. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | Reported FY24-FY26 revenue rose 1328.49→3035.71 lakh, only 2 annual intervals [S1 p 291]. FY24 merger, FY25 packaging takeovers and Aug 25 price rise obscure organic same-perimeter demand [A03;S1 p 339]. No reliable 3/5 year organic CAGR, volumes, same-store sales or acquired contribution; do not award 9 from nominal 2 year CAGR. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | Additional B2B showroom and B2C floors provide concrete distribution avenues; plating design 8064 kg/year [S1 pp 145-157]. West Bengal 65.77% and eastern states 86.32% FY26; no orders or independent plant appraisal [S1 pp 39-49,131]. Contrary: affordable imitation can substitute expensive gold, but accessible share/units and showroom mature cohorts are ND. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | Incremental finance-side capital proxy and normalized operating earnings can be calculated [S1 pp 290-303]. Property investment, acquired businesses and proposed 8.8037 crore plating facility do not provide matched project returns [A02,A03,S1 p 145]. Accounting-return proxy is not a verified incremental cohort ROIC; cost per kg, volumes, mix, unit contribution and sustainable maintenance capex missing. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Medium | Three audited/restated annual CFO sums negative 90.79 lakh against PAT 923.19 lakh [S1 p 292]. Receivable ageing and inventory cycle worsen into June; supplier credit no longer offsets growth [A04]. Contrary: showroom stocking has a stated commercial reason and IPO funds it, but negative conversion persists over 3 years; not merely one transient quarter. |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | June cash 0.2495 crore vs promoter loan 3.064 crore; no bank line [S1 pp 300,353]. IPO gross 34.99104 crore verified from shares and issue price; objects absorb 27.4681 crore before issue costs/general purposes [S2,S1 pp 129-130]. Current post-IPO bank balance, exact net proceeds, deployment and promoter loan due dates are not verified; projections are not funding commitments. |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | B2C ground floor opened April 2026 and existing B2B extended;37 new customers reported FY26 [S1 pp 133,334]. IPO completed 12 Oct and expansion has dated civil/retail schedule [S2,S1 pp 149-156]. Contrary: plant approvals/machine orders and showroom definitive acquisition pending at RHP date; no long promises-vs-delivery record. |\\n| EARN | 4 | Medium | Core operating EBIT improved from loss FY24 to profit FY25/FY26 [S1 p 291]. FY26 other income 1.1846 crore includes 1.1795 crore investment gain; FY24 similarly gain-dependent [S1 p 301]. Contrary: FY26 normalized operating profit remains positive, but price/mix/margins changed and post-IPO shares dilute historical EPS; June cannot be annualized. |\\n| R | NS | Low | Complete monthly conditional model includes capital requirements and funded/unfunded branches. Actual entry cash and final net proceeds are not known; conditional returns are not supported forecasts. No favorable return score may be assigned by replacing unknown capital position with optional finance. |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | Linked bear combines weaker sales/margins, aged collections, opening inventory and delayed overbudget plant. Cash-floor breach can precede annual profit recognition; bounded optional equity can run out. Contrary: fresh IPO capital and tangible property give potential protection, but restricted uses and illiquid assets do not guarantee recovery. |\\n| P | 3 | Medium | Current price 68.60 implies marketcap 106.44499 crore; normalized earnings multiple exceeds reported multiple. Reverse/hurdle solutions demand stronger realization or terminal economics than conservative cash-linked base. Contrary: a high-execution branch can create value, but not enough evidence for an expectation score above mixed. |\\n| L | NS | Low | Only 6 possible exchange sessions since 12 Oct; actual issuer rows only 12,13,19 Oct [S3]. 19 Oct turnover 109760 rupees and 1600 shares; stock ST trade-for-trade and 1600 lot [S2-S4]. 20/60 session means, median including verified zero days, spread and executable depth unavailable; pre-listing dates are NA, absent rows ND. |\\n\\n\\n| Measure | Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | GOV | 0.20 | 4 | 0.80 |\\n| BQS | MOAT | 0.20 | 4 | 0.80 |\\n| BQS | REV | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | DUR | 0.10 | 5 | 0.50 |\\n| BQS | CAP | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | CASH | 0.10 | 2 | 0.20 |\\n| BQS | BAL | 0.08 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | EXEC | 0.08 | 5 | 0.40 |\\n| GQS | REV | 0.40 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | DUR | 0.20 | 5 | 1.00 |\\n| GQS | EARN | 0.15 | 4 | 0.60 |\\n| GQS | CAP | 0.15 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | CASH | 0.10 | 2 | 0.20 |\\n| IAS | BQS | 0.25 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | R | 0.35 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | D | 0.20 | 3 | 0.60 |\\n| IAS | P | 0.10 | 3 | 0.30 |\\n| IAS | L | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n\\n\\nOne-point uncertainty tests preserve missing-data weights. A hypothetical GOV fall from 4 to 3 activates the framework’s BQS/IAS cap at 3; it is not a finding of substantiated misconduct in this case.\\n\\n| Factor change | BQS raw bound | BQS effective bound | IAS effective bound | Governance override |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV -1 | 2.82–5.70 | 2.82–3.00 | 1.60–3.00 | True |\\n| GOV 1 | 3.22–6.10 | 3.22–6.10 | 1.60–7.90 | False |\\n| MOAT -1 | 2.82–5.70 | 2.82–5.70 | 1.60–7.90 | False |\\n| MOAT 1 | 3.22–6.10 | 3.22–6.10 | 1.60–7.90 | False |\\n\\n\\nMoving GOV and MOAT together by one point changes the BQS known weighted contribution by 0.40 in either direction; it does not fill the missing 32% evidence weight. Uncertainty therefore dominates tiny apparent ranking differences. REV is not assigned from the two-year reported CAGR, and the missing organic evidence is not replaced with a neutral score.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nCity Girl and Manikya cover wedding, regular-wear and imitation CZ/AD jewellery. The company coordinates design, contract manufacturers, plating, quality checks, packaging and distribution; Karigars source and work on metal mountings. The RHP calls the jewellery line manufactured goods, but the economics are outsourced manufacturing, not simple gold-jewellery resale and not an owned integrated factory. Cosmetics are purchased branded goods for trading. The third segment comprises hotel-related services, commission and rents; FY24 included food/lodging while subsequent periods emphasize leasing. [S1 pp197–227, 300–312]\\n\\nFY26 segment revenues are ₹24.8572 crore jewellery, ₹4.9026 crore cosmetics and ₹0.5973 crore hotel-related income. In April–June 2026, jewellery is ₹7.3586 crore, cosmetics zero and rentals ₹0.1342 crore. Do not interpret zero quarterly cosmetics revenue as confirmed permanent discontinuation. The June operating and channel tables do not all use the same denominator. The audited/restated AS17 segment table is the anchor; inconsistent marketing tables are not forced into a fictitious exact driver bridge. [S1 pp301, 312; A07]\\n\\nFY26 wholesale contributed 82.53% versus 55.99% in FY24; June wholesale is 90.66%. The strategy says B2C expansion, but realized mix has moved toward wholesale so far. FY26 West Bengal supplied 65.77% of revenue and four eastern states 86.32%; June eastern concentration rose to 91.71%. FY26 top-five customers are 11.11% and top-ten 15.15%, meaning geographic and channel concentration are more acute than a single named customer's dominance. Counterparties are anonymized; connectedness cannot be independently ruled out. The FY25 top-ten amount divided by total revenue is 23.20%, inconsistent with the printed 20.24%. [S1 pp39–48]\\n\\nSales to export merchants are not equivalent to direct foreign-currency export revenue. The customer-type table shows FY26 ₹1.3148 crore to export merchants against its own ₹28.291 crore denominator, while the geography table describes domestic states. Import CIF and foreign-exchange expenditure are nil in the notes; no foreign-currency borrowing was identified. Direct export destinations, invoice currencies and hedging are ND. Currency risk is principally an indirect commodity/input-price exposure on the evidence available, not a verified dollar-revenue hedge. [S1 pp215, 307–308]\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\nThe plausible advantage is local distribution, familiar designs, replenishment breadth and quality coordination. Relationships, a six-month colour guarantee and frequent collections may help repeat orders. They do not prove consumer brand pricing power, switching costs, exclusive production or superior lifetime customer economics. Karigar agreements are short, nonexclusive and have no noncompete. The company produces against demand expectations, not binding long-term order commitments. FY26 top-ten contract manufacturers supplied 35.46% of the stated purchases and June 61.37%, while the broader supplier table uses another denominator; these are separate populations, not interchangeable concentration ratios. [S1 pp41–49, 211–227]\\n\\nThe peer cross-check is economic, not a copied sector P/E target:\\n\\n- Banaras Beads is the RHP's listed comparison and has beads/fashion-accessory manufacturing and wholesale/retail-customer exposure. Its export orientation and product economics differ from a Kolkata imitation-jewellery distributor. The RHP itself says strict comparability is limited. [S1 pp165–166; P1]\\n- Vaibhav Global is a useful distribution/control benchmark because it has international TV/digital retail and vertically integrated fashion-jewellery operations. Its owned channels, customer data, geography and scale are materially different. It is not a valid plug-in multiple. [P2]\\n- Goldiam illustrates a different jewellery model: export-led natural/lab-grown diamond products and customer-held consignment inventory. Its metal value, foreign exposure and working-capital risks differ; its operating margins do not validate RK's margins. [P3]\\n\\nNo current peer valuation ranking is claimed. Promotional claims of national leadership or a large total market do not establish RK's accessible share. There is no evidence here for MOAT ≥7.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\nReported operations revenue rose from ₹13.2849 crore in FY24 to ₹17.7719 crore in FY25 and ₹30.3571 crore in FY26. Annual additions were ₹4.4870 crore and ₹12.5852 crore. Only three annual observations exist, hence only two growth intervals. A three-year CAGR requires four observations; five and ten years require six and eleven. Those endpoints were not located. The mathematically valid two-year reported CAGR is shown in the calculation table, not mislabeled organic or five-year growth. [S1 p291]\\n\\nThe bridge is not purely volume. Jewellery grew ₹12.0388 crore in FY26, cosmetics ₹0.4670 crore and hotel-related income ₹0.0794 crore, within source rounding. Management attributes growth to customer additions, mix and a 12–15% selling-price increase from August 2025. It reports 37 new customers and ₹5.5116 crore from those customers in FY26, but that amount is not automatically the incremental revenue versus the prior year because existing and new cohorts have different comparison bases. Unit volumes, weight per item and like-for-like store sales are unavailable. [S1 pp334–339]\\n\\nFY24 incorporated two merged companies and FY25 two packaging proprietorships; acquired revenue is not separated. The 1 April 2022 appointed merger date differs from the 1 April 2023 accounting incorporation described in note59. Reported FY25/FY26 comparisons can be described, but cannot establish a clean three-year organic demand history. June product mix is heavily wedding jewellery, unlike FY26's regular-wear majority, so annualizing that quarter would create false confidence. Online order counts of 65,994/61,951/63,665 for FY24/FY25/FY26 do not substantiate the broad narrative of fast digital scaling. [S1 pp213, 303, 309, 333–339]\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nThe actual pipeline comprises an Ezra Street B2B showroom, further Rash Behari Avenue B2C floors and a Baruipur plating facility. It is not an unlimited national rollout. Management's ₹47.50/₹72.375 crore FY27/FY28 revenue assumptions are plans, not audited guidance achieved. The RHP contains inconsistent B2C descriptions: G+4, three-storied, ground/first floor and two/three additional selling floors. It also quotes ₹2.40 crore versus ₹1.60 crore of incremental B2C inventory in different explanations. The model uses the formal ₹5.60 crore total issue inventory object and does not manufacture a store-productivity forecast from those inconsistent descriptions. [S1 pp130–157]\\n\\nFour proposed PVD machines are specified at one kg/hour, seven productive hours/day, 288 working days/year: 8,064 kg/year. The ₹8.8037 crore funded plant cost excludes gifted land valued at ₹0.29 crore; ₹9.0937 crore is the stated full land-plus-project capital. It is not all machine cost: civil construction is ₹5.8302 crore and four machines ₹2.9735 crore. Completion is intended for October 2027, conditional on IPO realization and approvals. No observed plant utilization, sale price per kg or savings per kg exists. [S1 pp145–150]\\n\\nAt 50%/75%/100% utilization, the facility would process 4,032/6,048/8,064 kg annually. A 15% pretax cash return on ₹8.8037 crore alone would require about ₹3,275/₹2,183/₹1,638 incremental annual cash contribution per kg respectively, before considering additional working capital, taxes and sustaining capex. These are hurdle arithmetic, not demonstrated unit economics. FY26 outsourced plating expense of ₹2.5562 crore is a gross ceiling on replaceable expense at the old scale, not a free saving: in-house labour, consumables, energy, compliance, maintenance and depreciation remain. The model does not simply add that entire expense to profit. [S1 pp303, 332–339]\\n\\nIncremental inventory is working capital even though the prospectus describes initial stocking as capital expenditure. The model counts it once inside ONWC, not once as inventory and again as depreciable capex. Expansion quotes are inclusive of taxes; adding GST again would double-count the cash need. Later growth has additional fixed-capital and maintenance requirements. GQS cannot be strong when real demand, cohort returns and cash conversion remain unverified.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\nThe Qasim/Usman family controls the issuer. The post-IPO promoter/promoter-group position is approximately 72.26%; exact share counts, rather than inconsistent rounded RHP percentages, govern the share bridge. The 9 October exchange filing declares no outstanding warrants, convertibles, ESOPs or promoter encumbrances. All 11,249,563 pre-IPO shares are locked until specified 2027–2029 dates. Promoter/group holdings are 11,211,772 (72.26%); public holdings are 4,304,991 (27.74%). The immediately unlocked IPO float is 4,267,200 shares (27.50%), not the same as the public percentage. Free IPO shares do not guarantee ready exit. [S2]\\n\\nA 16:1 bonus in March 2026 changes share units, not economic ownership. The May 2025 2,223-share allotment settled a takeover liability at face value plus ₹262 premium, and must not be mistaken for cash proceeds. The October IPO is genuine fresh dilution: pre-issue ownership is spread over 15,516,763 shares instead of 11,249,563. The exact equity-growth and financing effects are separate from historical EPS. [S1 pp298, 303; S2]\\n\\nThe related-party loan from Mohammed Usman increased from ₹1.7032 crore in March to ₹3.0640 crore in June, interest free. That supported operations, but no irrevocable future funding or verified standstill is assumed. FY26 director remuneration disclosed in the RPT schedule is small relative to PAT; that does not eliminate larger capital-allocation issues. FY24 related advances and merged entities, MK Jewellers purchases and unmatched related-party balances deserve confirmation. No evidence reviewed substantiates diversion. [S1 pp304–305, 353]\\n\\n| Dated commitment / milestone | Verified position at cutoff | Treatment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Ground-floor B2C opening, April 2026 | RHP states operating; separate mature-store economics unavailable | Delivered operating milestone, not proof of return |\\n| IPO/listing, October 2026 | Official listing and shares verified | Completed capital-market milestone |\\n| Ezra showroom, February–March 2027 | MOU, definitive acquisition not executed at RHP date | Pending, not missed yet |\\n| B2C fit-out, March 2027 | Tax-inclusive budget and staged works; inconsistent floor descriptions | Pending; require final scope |\\n| Baruipur plating, October 2027 | Land available, approvals and construction/machinery execution outstanding | Pending; model includes delay/overrun |\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\nThe following values are ₹crore, converted from source ₹lakh. June is three months, not a full year. “Post-IPO equivalent EPS” divides historical profit by current shares for valuation comparability; it is not the historical accounting EPS. Other-income-adjusted reported PAT uses a disclosed 25% analytical tax on removed other income, not a claimed tax restatement; fully normalized core PAT is shown separately. Normalized operating EBIT removes all other income and adds finance cost; operating EBITDA then adds D&A. Source KPI EBITDA sometimes includes other income or omits the small finance add-back, hence the definition is rebuilt rather than copied. [S1 pp290–314]\\n\\n| ₹ crore, except EPS | FY24 | FY25 | FY26 | Apr–Jun 26 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Revenue | 13.2849 | 17.7719 | 30.3571 | 7.4928 |\\n| Jewellery | 8.9571 | 12.8184 | 24.8572 | 7.3586 |\\n| Cosmetics | 3.3567 | 4.4356 | 4.9026 | 0.0000 |\\n| Hotel | 0.9711 | 0.5179 | 0.5973 | 0.1342 |\\n| Other Income | 1.1725 | 0.0507 | 1.1846 | 0.0017 |\\n| Operating Ebitda | 0.1171 | 2.9779 | 7.3448 | 2.5551 |\\n| Operating Ebit | -0.1443 | 2.7500 | 7.1218 | 2.4759 |\\n| Pat | 0.9478 | 1.9972 | 6.2869 | 1.8286 |\\n| Other-income-adjusted reported PAT | 0.0684 | 1.9592 | 5.3984 | 1.8273 |\\n| Core PAT, no loss tax shield | -0.1476 | 2.0607 | 5.3341 | 1.8559 |\\n| CFO | -0.2610 | 0.0309 | -0.6778 | -0.3539 |\\n| Cash Capex | 1.1879 | 0.0032 | 0.3635 | 0.0467 |\\n| Reported CFO minus cash capex | -1.4489 | 0.0277 | -1.0413 | -0.4006 |\\n| Debt | 0.6090 | 0.6088 | 1.7032 | 3.0640 |\\n| Cash | 0.3406 | 0.1408 | 0.1291 | 0.2495 |\\n| Equity | 7.8177 | 9.8149 | 16.1622 | 17.9908 |\\n| Investment Property | 0.2910 | 0.3414 | 5.8563 | 6.6960 |\\n| Financial Investments | 3.9966 | 4.0242 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 |\\n| Trade receivables | 0.4850 | 1.5587 | 3.4193 | 5.6843 |\\n| Inventory | 1.0861 | 5.0931 | 7.8287 | 9.5581 |\\n| Trade payables | 1.8774 | 5.0330 | 3.8800 | 4.1745 |\\n| Finance-side capital proxy | 4.0895 | 6.2587 | 17.7363 | 20.8053 |\\n| Reported EPS on post-IPO shares | 0.6108 | 1.2871 | 4.0517 | 1.1785 |\\n\\n\\nThe series labeled normalized_pat_tax_assumed in the machine-readable table is only other-income-adjusted reported PAT, not fully normalized earnings: FY24 remains ₹0.0684 crore because the original deferred-tax benefit is retained. The separate fully_normalized_pat_no_loss_shield series charges 25% on positive core pretax profit and allows no tax shield for losses; FY24 is negative ₹0.1476 crore. Core EBIT is negative ₹0.1443 crore. This distinction prevents a false positive core-profit base.\\n\\n| Calculated metric | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Cumulative Cfo | -0.9079 |\\n| Cumulative Pat | 9.2319 |\\n| Cumulative Fcf Proxy | -2.4625 |\\n| Reported Two Year Cagr | 0.5116 |\\n| Cumulative Cfo Pat | -0.0983 |\\n| Marketcap | 106.4450 |\\n| Pe Reported | 16.9312 |\\n| Pe Normalized Tax Assumed | 19.7177 |\\n| Historical Ev Stale Not Postipo | 108.0191 |\\n| Ipo Gross | 34.9910 |\\n| Postipo Dilution Existing Fraction | 0.2750 |\\n| June Finance Capital Proxy | 20.8053 |\\n| June Operating Asset Bridge | 21.6091 |\\n| June Tax Liabilities Bridge | 0.8038 |\\n| June Bridge Rounding Difference | 0.0000 |\\n| Fy25 Top10 Recalculated Pct | 23.2046 |\\n| June Aged Over 6M Pct | 17.9019 |\\n\\n\\nThe ratio of three-year summed CFO to summed PAT is negative; it is not the average of annual conversion ratios. FY26 pre-working-capital cash profit of ₹7.3396 crore was reduced by receivables, inventory, lower trade credit and advances, before cash taxes, to negative CFO. Capex is an additional investing use and cannot explain away weak operating cash. Cash capex FCF shown above is only a reported proxy; investment-property spending is separately embedded in the investment section and increases the broader reinvestment burden. FY24's noncash-looking merger lines prevent an unqualified reconstructed total-cash-capex series. [S1 pp44–47, 292]\\n\\nThe finance-side capital proxy is equity plus borrowings less cash and genuinely financial investments, keeping investment property inside the business. June gross operating assets less operating liabilities are ₹21.6091 crore; deferred/current tax liabilities of ₹0.8038 crore bridge to ₹20.8053 crore finance-side capital, subject to disclosure rounding. This is not identical to precisely normalized operating IC because supplier/government advances, property classification, tax balances and acquired entities require further reconciliation. FY25–FY26 normalized NOPAT changes and capital changes are reproducibly calculated, but are not sufficient to score CAP as a verified project ROIC. Average-capital returns and incremental returns have different denominators and are not confused.\\n\\n| Period | Δ NOPAT | Δ IC proxy | Incremental return | Average IC | NOPAT/avg IC | DSO | DIO | DPO proxy | CCC proxy |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY25 | 2.21 | 2.17 | 101.73% | 5.17 | 39.86% | 21.0 | 88.9 | 99.5 | 10.5 |\\n| FY26 | 3.28 | 11.48 | 28.57% | 12.00 | 44.52% | 29.9 | 140.1 | 96.7 | 73.4 |\\n\\n\\nDPO uses average trade payables divided by materials plus traded purchases plus inventory change, a compatible-cost proxy rather than verified credit purchases. DSO uses revenue; DIO uses the same cost proxy. CCC = DSO + DIO − DPO. Acquisition scope, property and noncash merger balances limit comparability. Minor ₹0.01 lakh differences between balance-sheet and ageing AP totals are treated as disclosure rounding; the ageing totals are used consistently.\\n\\nReceivables over six months increase from approximately ₹0.0236 crore in March to ₹1.0176 crore in June, including ₹0.1330 crore aged one to two years, with no doubtful allowance shown. Total inventory reaches ₹9.5581 crore, of which finished goods ₹6.6869 crore. The company's reported inventory days rise 38 → 85 → 117 → 205; receivable days 16 → 21 → 30 → 55. Some published cycle figures disagree (167 versus 179 days for June). Ratios computed from average balances and material-cost denominators are separately labeled; they are not substituted for the company's differing holding-period definitions. [S1 pp134–138, 300, 310–311]\\n\\nFull audit and disclosure reconciliation register:\\n\\n**A01 · S1 pp287-289**\\n\\nRead complete restatement examination report of Murarka & Associates, dated24 September2026. It says no qualifications requiring restatement adjustments. It explicitly is not a reissued original audit opinion. Original statutory reports, CARO and IFC annexures FY24-FY26 were not located in issuer investor index or public primary searches. Original-audit completeness remains ND, not clean assurance.\\n\\n**A02 · S1 pp292,298,303,309**\\n\\nCFS FY24 has a 2023 column header despite matching FY24 amounts. FY24 includes merger fixed assets−207.09lakh, merger reserves/P&L19.07lakh, deferred-tax−1.02lakh, share-capital33.35lakh and premium342.65lakh. Notes identify share-exchange amalgamation, not cash financing. FY26 CFS includes6.04lakh capital/premium although note3 describes2223 shares against a604993rupee takeover liability. Do not infer these lines are cash inflows/outflows. Preserve reported cash bridges but original bank-level and noncash reconciliation required; cannot safely restate only one leg.\\n\\n**A03 · S1 pp298,303,309**\\n\\nMerger shares differ between note3/30 (249128 plus84388) and note59 (270200 plus84377). Official post-IPO issued count is15516763 and controls current valuation. Historical merger economics and complete historic diluted-share bridge unresolved; bonus16:1 is not economic dilution.\\n\\n**A04 · S1 pp299-300,307-310**\\n\\nNoncurrent investments669.60lakh at June are investment in property. Cash24.95lakh includes15.78lakh cash in hand and9.17lakh bank. Receivables568.43lakh include88.46lakh aged6-12months and13.30lakh aged1-2years; no doubtful allowance. Note51 says receivables/payables/advances subject to confirmation. Do not net property against debt or use it for monthly cash rescue. Collection deterioration matters even with unmodified restatement wording.\\n\\n**A05 · S1 pp304-305,307**\\n\\nRPT loan from Mohammed Usman rose170.32 to306.40lakh in Q1; interest free. FY26 related MK Jewellers purchases72.06lakh, closing36.88lakh; June purchases9.13lakh, closing29.98lakh. FY24 Shaistah Usman advance314lakh both advanced/repaid. MD Aurangzeb advance5.75lakh in RPT differs from aggregate related-advance1.70lakh at June. No diversion proved. Need counterparty-confirmed reconciliation and contractual repayment/standstill terms. Historical gross RPT flows are not annual expense totals.\\n\\n**A06 · S1 pp307,309 and355-357**\\n\\nDetailed litigation table at printed357 totals34.19lakh for three tax matters; note33 components32.95+1.23 total34.18lakh, a0.01lakh rounding difference; older summary33.23lakh is stale. No criminal/regulatory promoter/company proceedings disclosed. Note43 says no outstanding loans but note5 and debt chapter show related-party debt. Use0.3419crore in linked tax-payment stress. Wording conflicts require reconciliation, not allegation.\\n\\n**A07 · S1 pp40-43,198-215,333-339**\\n\\nFY25 top10 customer412.39lakh divided by1777.19lakh equals23.20%, not20.24% printed. Customer tenure narrative4.77years differs table6.6. June segment/channel denominators differ. MD&A June PAT188.86lakh conflicts financial182.86lakh. Use financial statements and distinguish inconsistent operating tables; no false precision in organic or concentration scoring.\\n\\n**A08 · S1 pp293-296,298-314**\\n\\nAll financial notes1-61 and receivable/payable ageing and AS17 segment annexures were reviewed. Indian GAAP AS/DivisionI; no subsidiaries; inventories management-certified; finished goods lower cost/NRV, RM/WIP at cost. No bank facilities, no declared dividends. Restated financial extraction is Medium confidence, financial reliability/growth/valuation conclusions Low pending original-audit and scope reconciliation.\\n\\n**A09 · S1 notes4 and9, printed298 and306**\\n\\nNote9 states58.58lakh previously uncharged depreciation was adjusted against reserves; note4 records86.09lakh prior-period expense. These are distinct from the examination report statement about audit qualifications. Original audits and period-by-period restatement schedules are needed to establish which originating periods bore these charges. No claim of complete clean original-audit reconciliation is made.\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\nThe market capitalization is price times the verified current issued share count. Reported and normalized FY26 P/E are shown above. Do not use ₹5.59 historical EPS with the expanded post-IPO share count. Do not describe the business as debt-free merely because there is no bank borrowing. A defensible current EV, current P/B and current FCF yield are NS: the old June cash/debt/balance sheet predates the IPO and substantial deployment. A stale EV can be calculated for illustration but is not presented as a live valuation multiple.\\n\\nThe model is deliberately conditional. Starting at observed June cash, it rolls monthly assumptions through the assumed 9 October settlement and the entry date without resetting opening cash. IPO gross proceeds of ₹34.99104 crore are verified by issue shares × ₹82; the RHP does not finalize expenses and net general-purpose cash. The central fee assumption is ₹3 crore, tested at ₹2/₹4.5 crore. The separate object pools are ₹16.6537 crore fixed projects, ₹5.60 crore opening inventory and ₹5.2144 crore working capital; the residual after fees is general cash. These pools are analytically earmarked, not freely available to pay any obligation. No change of objects is assumed. No property is treated as cash.\\n\\nPre-entry timing assumes 60% of modeled monthly net operating cash precedes month-end requirements and 40% follows. Post-entry monthly requirements are conservatively paid before that month's operating cash. The full event ledger records both stages, cash floors and debt repayments. These timing assumptions must be replaced by actual bank and deployment records before R is scoreable. The base does not simply copy management's 50%-plus growth targets: revenue steps to ₹40/48/56/64/70 crore with EBITDA margins 18%/19% thereafter. The bear has demand loss, lower margins, more ONWC, a one-year plant delay and 25% cost overrun; the bull requires higher sales and maintained margins. [Model assumptions A]\\n\\nThe primary conditional terminal enterprise value is next-year FCFF divided by the 15% discount rate less 4% growth. This ties terminal value to paid-for capital and earned cash returns, rather than assuming that a market multiple is deserved. The implied EBITDA multiple is an output, not an input. Cash above both the operating floor and liquidity reserve is added, debt is deducted, and fully diluted future shares determine per-share value.\\n\\n| Case | Optional rescue | Year-5 sales | Earned EV | Implied EBITDA multiple | Earned value/share | Funded conditional CAGR | First breach | New shares |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | False | 36.00 | 12.47 | 2.89 | 10.22 | NS | 2026-07-31 preentry_requirements | 0 |\\n| bear | True | 36.00 | 12.47 | 2.89 | 10.22 | NS | 2026-07-31 preentry_requirements | 0 |\\n| base | False | 70.00 | 61.46 | 4.62 | 55.75 | -4.06 | None | 0 |\\n| base | True | 70.00 | 61.46 | 4.62 | 55.75 | -4.06 | None | 0 |\\n| bull | False | 112.00 | 122.57 | 4.76 | 78.99 | NS | 2031-10-19 terminal_capital_catchup | 0 |\\n| bull | True | 112.00 | 122.57 | 4.76 | 74.81 | 1.75 | None | 868218 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | False | 70.00 | 61.95 | 4.66 | 54.46 | -4.51 | None | 0 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | True | 70.00 | 61.95 | 4.66 | 54.46 | -4.51 | None | 0 |\\n\\n\\nA value labeled “terminal arithmetic” after a breach is not a realizable price target or ordinary investor return. The ordinary return and hurdle entry are NS after the first unfinanced cash-floor breach, even if subsequent counterfactual profits would refill cash. This prevents a false high return from a business that could not pay its earlier obligations. The optional equity branch is explicitly hypothetical: price ₹54.88, 3% fees, at most ₹8 crore gross and at most 1,483,237 new whole shares, also constrained by the current 17,000,000-share authorized ceiling. At the terminal capital event, any hypothetical rescue also prefunds the following month’s reinvestment and maintenance requirement so that raising merely to the cash floor cannot create a false funded terminal state. It cannot cure a pre-entry breach and it is not committed financing. Issuance approvals and buyers are not inferred from legal headroom.\\n\\n| Case, no rescue | Year | Sales | NOPAT | CFO | Capex | ΔONWC | Closing cash | Closing debt | Closing fixed | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | 1 | 30.00 | 1.81 | -5.31 | 0.75 | 5.60 | -0.15 | 0.00 | 11.81 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| bear | 2 | 28.00 | 1.19 | 2.12 | 14.58 | 0.00 | 1.27 | 0.00 | 25.45 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| bear | 3 | 30.00 | 1.33 | 2.86 | 7.89 | 0.00 | -0.99 | 0.00 | 31.81 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| bear | 4 | 33.00 | 1.78 | 3.37 | 1.12 | 0.00 | 1.25 | 0.00 | 31.36 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| bear | 5 | 36.00 | 2.07 | 3.63 | 1.20 | 0.00 | 3.68 | 0.00 | 30.99 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| base | 1 | 40.00 | 4.77 | -0.54 | 11.90 | 5.60 | 9.39 | 3.06 | 22.34 | conditional_funded |\\n| base | 2 | 48.00 | 5.85 | 7.17 | 6.51 | 0.00 | 12.54 | 0.00 | 27.53 | conditional_funded |\\n| base | 3 | 56.00 | 6.94 | 8.33 | 1.76 | 0.00 | 19.10 | 0.00 | 27.90 | conditional_funded |\\n| base | 4 | 64.00 | 8.06 | 6.93 | 1.92 | 2.55 | 26.66 | 0.00 | 28.41 | conditional_funded |\\n| base | 5 | 70.00 | 8.90 | 0.71 | 3.81 | 9.62 | 25.48 | 0.00 | 30.78 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 1 | 47.50 | 7.20 | 1.90 | 12.05 | 5.60 | 12.19 | 3.06 | 22.49 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 2 | 65.00 | 10.21 | 11.10 | 6.85 | 0.45 | 19.37 | 0.00 | 28.00 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 3 | 81.00 | 12.89 | 9.85 | 2.90 | 4.48 | 30.81 | 0.00 | 29.46 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 4 | 97.00 | 15.59 | 12.63 | 3.22 | 4.48 | 40.41 | 0.00 | 31.16 | conditional_funded |\\n| bull | 5 | 112.00 | 18.11 | -28.35 | 15.98 | 48.08 | -3.92 | 0.00 | 45.53 | unfunded_counterfactual |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 1 | 40.00 | 4.77 | -3.36 | 11.90 | 6.87 | 7.84 | 3.06 | 22.34 | conditional_funded |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 2 | 48.00 | 5.85 | 3.33 | 6.51 | 3.84 | 10.98 | 0.00 | 27.53 | conditional_funded |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 3 | 56.00 | 6.94 | 4.49 | 1.76 | 3.84 | 13.71 | 0.00 | 27.90 | conditional_funded |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 4 | 64.00 | 8.06 | 5.63 | 1.92 | 3.84 | 17.42 | 0.00 | 28.41 | conditional_funded |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 5 | 70.00 | 8.90 | 7.45 | 1.88 | 2.88 | 22.99 | 0.00 | 28.86 | conditional_funded |\\n\\n\\nThe following 5×/8×/10× EBITDA exits are secondary assumed market-multiple sensitivities, not the central earned-value forecast. Any funded CAGR here is a conditional market-price outcome, not the primary cash-earned result.\\n\\n| Case | Optional rescue | Year-5 sales | Year-5 EBITDA | Minimum cash | First breach | New shares | Terminal arithmetic/share | Funded conditional CAGR | 15% hurdle entry |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | False | 36.00 | 4.32 | -2.19 | 2026-07-31 preentry_requirements | 0 | 16.10 | NS% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 36.00 | 4.32 | -2.19 | 2026-07-31 preentry_requirements | 0 | 16.10 | NS% | NS |\\n| base | False | 70.00 | 13.30 | 0.13 | None | 0 | 84.71 | 4.31% | 42.12 |\\n| base | True | 70.00 | 13.30 | 0.13 | None | 0 | 84.71 | 4.31% | 42.12 |\\n| bull | False | 112.00 | 25.76 | -3.92 | 2031-10-19 terminal_capital_catchup | 0 | 166.01 | NS% | NS |\\n| bull | True | 112.00 | 25.76 | 0.24 | None | 868218 | 157.22 | 18.04% | 78.16 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | False | 70.00 | 13.30 | 0.13 | None | 0 | 83.11 | 3.91% | 41.32 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | True | 70.00 | 13.30 | 0.13 | None | 0 | 83.11 | 3.91% | 41.32 |\\n\\n\\nTerminal EV/EBITDA is an equity-return cross-check, not a forecast that the market will award that multiple. Debt is subtracted, only unrestricted excess cash above the floor and next-month operating-liquidity reserve is added, and operating property is not added a second time. There are no dividends, so retained cash is not counted again as distributions. Five-year total returns exclude investor taxes and transaction/market-impact costs. A 5% exit haircut would reduce annualized gross wealth return by a factor of 0.95^(1/5), roughly 1.02% of the gross annual wealth factor; actual impact may be much larger in one-sided SME trading.\\n\\nA second, FCFF valuation discounts monthly-aggregated annual NOPAT + D&A − cash capex − ΔONWC. Terminal growth is 4%, below each 12%/15%/18% discount rate. Next-year reinvestment is the greater of sales-based ONWC/fixed growth and NOPAT × growth / 15% terminal ROIC. Before valuation, missing opening capital needed to earn that ROIC on next-year average capital is paid from unrestricted cash, with depreciation feedback solved algebraically; excess existing capital is never released for free. Maintenance equals terminal D&A only as an explicit steady-state assumption, not a historical fact. A twelve-month continuation tests capital and maintenance payments before operating receipts, without adding those future cash receipts to year-five equity. Terminal capital, cash, debt and shares are preserved in the canonical record. Discount rates are analytical, not a claimed empirically measured WACC; the 15% equity hurdle is a separate assumption.\\n\\n| Case | Discount | EV | Terminal PV share, % | Per-share equity | Validity |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | 0.12 | -6.38 | -152.45 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| bear | 0.15 | -9.30 | -66.67 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| bear | 0.18 | -10.64 | -40.27 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| base | 0.12 | 43.47 | 110.32 | 43.56 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| base | 0.15 | 25.88 | 118.09 | 31.63 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| base | 0.18 | 16.26 | 129.80 | 24.89 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| bull | 0.12 | 75.72 | 126.29 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| bull | 0.15 | 43.23 | 140.96 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| bull | 0.18 | 26.25 | 160.37 | NS | NS: unsupported realizable value after cash breach |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 0.12 | 39.63 | 121.95 | 42.04 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 0.15 | 21.81 | 141.23 | 30.03 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | 0.18 | 12.07 | 176.31 | 23.27 | Conditional FCFF cross-check; all funding and terminal capital retained |\\n\\n\\nPrimary earned-value hurdle entry prices:\\n\\n| Case | Rescue | 12% entry | 15% entry | 18% entry |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | False | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bear | True | NS | NS | NS |\\n| base | False | 31.64 | 27.72 | 24.37 |\\n| base | True | 31.64 | 27.72 | 24.37 |\\n| bull | False | NS | NS | NS |\\n| bull | True | 42.45 | 37.19 | 32.70 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | False | 30.90 | 27.08 | 23.81 |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | True | 30.90 | 27.08 | 23.81 |\\n\\n\\n| Case | Rescue | Capital before | Catch-up paid | Capital after | Next NOPAT | Next reinvestment | Earned ROIC | Continuation breach |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear | False | 48.64 | 0.00 | 48.64 | 2.21 | 0.84 | 4.50 | None |\\n| bear | True | 48.64 | 0.00 | 48.64 | 2.21 | 0.84 | 4.50 | None |\\n| base | False | 50.61 | 9.63 | 60.24 | 9.22 | 2.46 | 15.00 | None |\\n| base | True | 50.61 | 9.63 | 60.24 | 9.22 | 2.46 | 15.00 | None |\\n| bull | False | 63.70 | 56.41 | 120.12 | 18.39 | 4.90 | 15.00 | 2032 terminal month1 requirements |\\n| bull | True | 63.70 | 56.41 | 120.12 | 18.39 | 4.90 | 15.00 | None |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | False | 61.81 | 0.00 | 61.81 | 9.29 | 2.48 | 14.74 | None |\\n| base_collection_funding_stress | True | 61.81 | 0.00 | 61.81 | 9.29 | 2.48 | 14.74 | None |\\n\\n\\nTerminal growth/discount cross-sensitivity, rerunning capital and funding at every growth assumption:\\n\\n| Terminal growth | Discount | Cash breach | Per current share |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 0.02 | 0.12 | None | 41.50 |\\n| 0.02 | 0.15 | None | 31.43 |\\n| 0.02 | 0.18 | None | 25.25 |\\n| 0.04 | 0.12 | None | 43.56 |\\n| 0.04 | 0.15 | None | 31.63 |\\n| 0.04 | 0.18 | None | 24.89 |\\n| 0.06 | 0.12 | None | 46.84 |\\n| 0.06 | 0.15 | None | 31.84 |\\n| 0.06 | 0.18 | None | 24.37 |\\n\\n\\nDCF values are conditional cross-checks, not a way around a cash breach. The DCF equity bridge adds entry unrestricted cash plus only the discounted value of actual eligible future pool draws, matched to the same annual timing used for FCFF, then deducts entry debt. It does not add the full opening earmark at par. Undrawn restricted cash has no credited value: in the base, ₹0.6339 crore remains restricted at the horizon and is not treated as distributable or freely repurposable. This is deliberately conservative rather than assuming a change of objects. Per-current-share DCF is suppressed for all rescue-enabled branches rather than ignoring future financiers’ claims; it is also suppressed after a funding breach. A high terminal-value share demonstrates sensitivity, not confidence.\\n\\nTwo-way value sensitivity, holding the base operating path except the stated dimensions:\\n\\n| Margin shift | Terminal multiple | Cash breach | Funded CAGR | Hurdle entry |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| -.03 | 6 | None | -2.11 | 30.65 |\\n| -.03 | 8 | None | 2.09 | 37.83 |\\n| -.03 | 10 | None | 5.70 | 45.01 |\\n| 0 | 6 | None | -0.30 | 33.59 |\\n| 0 | 8 | None | 4.31 | 42.12 |\\n| 0 | 10 | None | 8.23 | 50.64 |\\n| .03 | 6 | None | 1.28 | 36.35 |\\n| .03 | 8 | None | 6.27 | 46.22 |\\n| .03 | 10 | None | 10.46 | 56.09 |\\n\\n\\nWorking-capital and issue-cost sensitivity, with complete monthly cash paths retained:\\n\\n| Extra ONWC/sales | IPO fees, ₹ crore | Cash breach | Min.cash | Funded CAGR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 0 | 2 | None | 0.13 | 4.47 |\\n| 0 | 3 | None | 0.13 | 4.31 |\\n| 0 | 4.5 | None | 0.13 | 4.07 |\\n| .08 | 2 | None | 0.13 | 4.52 |\\n| .08 | 3 | None | 0.13 | 4.36 |\\n| .08 | 4.5 | None | 0.13 | 4.13 |\\n| .16 | 2 | None | 0.13 | 4.32 |\\n| .16 | 3 | None | 0.13 | 4.16 |\\n| .16 | 4.5 | None | 0.13 | 3.92 |\\n\\n\\nThe June current-tax provision of ₹0.5518 crore is paid in projection month one separately from forward tax. Deferred tax of ₹0.2520 crore is not a cash payable. Bear and the separate base collection/funding stress pay ₹0.3419 crore for the detailed litigation-table tax contingency and absorb ₹1.21021 crore of collection/stock-replacement cash loss in month three (25% of June receivables older than six months plus 10% of June inventory). The latter is an analytical loss-and-replenishment stress, not an allegation or a booked impairment. The stress raises ONWC intensity by 16 percentage points and runs both no-rescue and bounded-rescue paths. The 18-month project-payment schedule is an analyst reschedule after entry, not the issuer’s fiscal-year object-utilization table.\\n\\nThe reverse test solves the constant revenue growth needed for price preservation or 12%/15%/18% annual equity hurdles, under base margins and capital intensity. The “earned” rows use the primary FCFF perpetuity; 6×/8×/10× rows are secondary market-multiple comparisons. A feasible solution must also survive every modeled cash event without rescue. It tests a one-percentage-point grid from 0–60% annual growth, refining valid local brackets. No-solution results apply to that declared search, not a proof that no mathematical solution exists anywhere. Every grid and bisection candidate has a complete deduplicated cash path in the canonical record. A funded threshold adjacent to an unfunded candidate is explicitly a funding boundary, not a continuous equality root.\\n\\n| Terminal multiple | Annual hurdle | Required revenue CAGR | Year-5 sales | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| earned | 0 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| earned | 12.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| earned | 15.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| earned | 18.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 6 | 0 | 20.40 | 76.81 | Conditional funded equality bracket; not demand/capacity validation |\\n| 6 | 12.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 6 | 15.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 6 | 18.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 8 | 0 | 11.39 | 52.07 | Conditional funded equality bracket; not demand/capacity validation |\\n| 8 | 12.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 8 | 15.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 8 | 18.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 10 | 0 | 7.19 | 42.95 | Conditional funded equality bracket; not demand/capacity validation |\\n| 10 | 12.00 | 25.43 | 94.24 | Conditional funded equality bracket; not demand/capacity validation |\\n| 10 | 15.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n| 10 | 18.00 | NS | NS | No funded solution on the declared 1-point grid from 0% to 60% growth |\\n\\n\\nThese solutions establish mathematical expectations, not verified accessible demand or plant capacity. No kg-to-revenue translation is claimed without product weight/realization data. The conditional hurdle-entry numbers can help frame price discipline but are not an actionable fair-value range while G2/G3/G4 remain unresolved.\\n\\nLiquidity is a separate risk. There are six possible post-listing sessions through 19 October and actual issuer trade rows on 12, 13 and 19 October. Absent rows on other dates are retained as absent/ND rather than silently invented zero records; pre-listing dates are NA. The 19 October trade is one 1,600-share lot with ₹109,760 turnover. Four lots cost ₹439,040; five cost ₹548,800, so a ₹5 lakh position cannot be matched exactly. Observed-only mean/median and 2%/5%/10% arithmetic are stored as incomplete illustrations, not the required 60-session liquidation test. Bid/ask spread and executable depth are ND. Therefore L and IAS remain NS. [S2–S4]\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\nThe decisive failure variable is conversion of fashionable, increasingly aged inventory and credit sales into unrestricted cash. A regional demand or fashion reversal can reduce sales while finished goods need markdowns; receivables age, suppliers require quicker payment, and the plant absorbs cash before delivering savings. Promoter repayment or missing bank access can make the timing fatal even with positive year-end earnings. An IPO does not make every future rupee spendable for every purpose. The shareholder can suffer economic loss through lower durable earnings, forced dilution, distressed disposal or inability to exit, not merely a temporary quotation decline.\\n\\nImpairment risk is High on this evidence. The contrary case is real: IPO proceeds, reported jewellery growth, modest top-customer concentration and property assets could allow a successful transition. But property realizability, title documents, net proceeds after sale, lease commitments and a liquid market have not been independently valued. No liquidation floor is invented. A hypothetical funding failure is not described as actual insolvency or misconduct.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\nA fivefold price in five years requires approximately 38% annual price growth; tenfold requires 58.5%. At unchanged shares, ₹68.60 becoming ₹343/₹686 requires equity values of roughly ₹532/₹1,064 crore before interim distributions. At 10× terminal EBITDA and ignoring a favorable net-cash offset, that means about ₹53/₹106 crore EBITDA, far beyond the present ₹7.34 crore operating EBITDA base. A 20% margin would imply approximately ₹266/₹532 crore sales, plus the matching inventory, fixed capital and dilution. No verified unit economics, accessible customer demand or funded store footprint supports that outcome. The fully linked scale tests below do not claim these sales are accessible. They apply constant growth to the approximate ₹266/₹532 crore scale, 19% first-year/20% later EBITDA margins and the same capital/funding rules, with and without bounded rescue. They do not convert an unfunded arithmetic target into an ordinary return.\\n\\n| Illustrative target | Sales CAGR | Year-5 sales | Rescue | First breach | New shares | Earned funded CAGR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 5× | 54.36 | 266 | False | 2031-10-19 terminal_capital_catchup | 0 | NS |\\n| 5× | 54.36 | 266 | True | 2031-10-19 terminal_capital_catchup | 1457725 | NS |\\n| 10× | 77.31 | 532 | False | 2030-01-19 requirements_before_receipts | 0 | NS |\\n| 10× | 77.31 | 532 | True | 2030-08-19 requirements_before_receipts | 1457725 | NS |\\n\\n\\nClassification: Speculative / unsupported. A lower terminal multiple or new shares makes the hurdle harder, not easier.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\nThese are research review triggers, not scheduled automations and not automatic trading rules.\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Warning / thesis-breaking evidence | Review source |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Audits and cash reconciliation | Restatement report only; noncash-looking merger/takeover cash lines | Unreconciled bank flows or scope limitation; material misstatement would overturn reliability | Original FY24–FY26 reports, CARO/IFC and bank reconciliations |\\n| Collections | June receivables ₹5.6843 crore; over-six-month ₹1.0176 crore | Further ageing without cash recovery; material write-off versus equity | Next interim/annual ageing and subsequent collections |\\n| Inventory productivity | June ₹9.5581 crore, finished goods ₹6.6869 crore | Stock rises faster than cash sales; markdowns eliminate presumed price/mix margin | Stock ageing, NRV policy and store-level sell-through |\\n| IPO cash and deployment | Gross ₹34.99104 crore; formal objects ₹27.4681 crore | Missing/changed uses, unavailable funds or unexplained RPT payments | Actual bank cash, issue expenses, utilization certificate |\\n| Related-party funding | June promoter loan ₹3.064 crore | Demand for repayment without demonstrated liquidity; new advance inconsistency | Agreements, confirmations and next balance sheet |\\n| B2B/B2C projects | Ezra target Feb–Mar27; B2C Mar27 | Definitive acquisition/approved scope delayed; no mature contribution despite working capital | Registered documents, fit-out completion and cohort economics |\\n| Plating | October27 target; 8,064 kg design capacity | Delay, overruns or low contribution per kg; returns below capital cost | Licences, commissioning, utilization and cost bridge |\\n| Organic demand | No verified volume/same-store bridge | Growth driven only by prices/acquired turnover or credit extension | Volumes, realization and customer cohorts |\\n| Tradability | ST; 1,600 lot; three actual trade rows | Repeated one-lot/one-price days, no executable bids | Official 20/60-session data and verified order-book conditions |\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\nAction category: research hold / pending evidence, not eligible for a numerical business, growth or investment ranking. This is not “avoid for proven governance abuse,” and it is not a verified current funding default. It fails the evidence-backed compounder designation because the requisite quality/growth evidence and several critical factors are not scoreable. No price alone cures missing audit, funding or execution-liquidity evidence. Once those are resolved, normalized margins, funded inventory needs and the actual entry cash balance should drive a fresh IAS. Neither the IPO price nor the original purchase cost, if any, determines intrinsic value.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\n- S1: [R.K. Fashion Accessories RHP](https://affinityglobalcap.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/RHP_LFINAL-1.pdf), 2026-09-29. Printed287-314 financial report and all notes;129-161 objects;39-72 risks;197-227 business;237-282 governance;318-353 MD&A and debt\\n- S2: [NSE listing circular CML76831 and post-issue shareholding](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/circulars/CML76831.zip), 2026-10-09. \\n- S3: [NSE UDiFF market data](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip), 2026-10-19. \\n- S4: [NSE security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz), 2026-10-19. \\n- S5: [Issuer investor and RHP index](https://citygirljewellery.co.in/rhpl), 2026-10-23 retrieval. \\n\\n- P1: [Banaras Beads official company overview](https://www.banarasbead.com/about-us/) and the issuer RHP's peer comparison. Structural comparison only; no live peer multiple is used.\\n- P2: [Vaibhav Global official history and business overview](https://www.vaibhavglobal.com/public/vgl-at-a-glance). Structural comparison only; undated current site text is not evidence of a specific historical valuation.\\n- P3: [Goldiam May 2025 investor presentation](https://www.goldiam.com/pdf/corporate-announcement/2025-26/Investors%20Presentation%2031-3-2025.pdf), business and consignment inventory discussion. Older dated comparison, not current target multiple.\\n\\nV denotes an observed primary disclosure; C a reproducible calculation; M a management statement; A an analyst assumption. Historical data and the security master are V, ratio/score/model arithmetic C, capacity targets and rollout milestones M, and all future cash timing, margins, fees, capital intensity, repayment timing, rescue terms and valuation multiples A. Material source locators are attached to the relevant paragraphs and input records. Source-file hashes authenticate the retrieved vintage; they do not validate management's underlying claims.\\n\\nOpen questions, in order: original statutory audit and annexures; exact noncash/cash financing reconciliation; related-party and merger-share conflicts; final prospectus and IPO expense/net-cash/deployment proof; promoter-loan repayment terms; acquired/price/volume revenue bridge; store-unit and per-kg plating economics; customer ageing recoveries; original inventory ageing/NRV support; and sufficient verified trading history. No public claim that the full original-audit history was read is made. Financial tables preserve source rounding rather than forcing contradictory tables to agree.\\n\\nThe compact and full reconstruction packages contain authored inputs, the report, canonical data and deterministic scripts. All scenario, monthly cash, sensitivity and every tested reverse-expectation row are represented in canonical data. The full package adds convenient redundant tables. Neither contains original PDFs, OCR, source-page images, private service identifiers, credentials or transport helpers. Rebuild instructions delete both outputs and the validation attestation; successful fresh-download reconstruction establishes artifact completeness and arithmetic, not investment truth.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"end\"","\"original_heading\"","415","439","1839","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","1888","4464","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","4502","12122","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","12146","14531","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","14562","16416","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","16445","18181","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","18224","20583","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","20624","23009","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","23035","33289","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","33328","51656","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","51695","52837","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","52865","54401","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","54424","56464","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","56513","57175","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","57220","60393","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"historical_inputs.json\"","\"d6559165b9bee114b8adea114d47275332d49cafab3659b52bd6f5efb606ac15\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"e4d7dfb3fb1d67e6a132bd58f9b396c3157ba22372b65ed78271bd0cc2ca4093\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not a bundled original filing. Source-access qualifications remain in the research.\"","\"content\"","\"model_inputs.json\"","\"2a650c7f076454907030a1e8769012114906d3bf9c132c71afd9ea59fe960dbf\"","\"ba9de65aabc4f08f236eb15dfb3f9c86a25a2b81de85